export const questions = [
{"no":1,"text":"Alam Khan, one of those who invited Babar to invade India, was:","A":"An uncle of Ibrahim Lodi and a pretender to the throne of Delhi","B":"A cousin of Ibrahim Lodi who was ill-treated and expelled from the country","C":"The father of Dilawar Khan to whom cruel treatment was meted out by Ibrahim Lodi","D":"A high official in Punjab province who was discontented with Ibrahim Lodi’s treatment of his tribe","ans":"A","category":"History","subcategory":"Medieval India","tags":["Mughal Invasion","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"Alam Khan was associated with the Lodi dynasty.\nHe was an uncle of Ibrahim Lodi.\nHe had ambitions for the throne of Delhi.\nHe was among those who invited Babur to intervene in India.\nBabur eventually defeated Ibrahim Lodi in the First Battle of Panipat, 1526.","revisionPoints":"Ibrahim Lodi → last ruler of the Lodi dynasty.\nFirst Battle of Panipat → 1526.\nBabur defeated Ibrahim Lodi.\nMughal rule in India began after the victory at Panipat."},
{"no":2,"text":"The Battle of Dharmat was fought between:","A":"Muhammad Ghori and Jai Chand","B":"Babar and the Afghans","C":"Aurangzeb and Dara Shikoh","D":"Ahmad Shah Durrani and the Marathas","ans":"C","category":"History","subcategory":"Medieval India","tags":["Mughal Wars of Succession","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"The Battle of Dharmat was fought in 1658.\nIt was part of the Mughal war of succession.\nAurangzeb and Murad's forces fought the forces supporting Dara Shikoh.\nThe battle took place near Ujjain.","revisionPoints":"Mughal War of Succession → after Shah Jahan's illness.\nDara Shikoh → eldest son of Shah Jahan.\nAurangzeb eventually became emperor.\nBattle of Dharmat → 1658."},
{"no":3,"text":"Which one among the following has the largest shipyard in India?","A":"Kolkata","B":"Kochi","C":"Mumbai","D":"Visakhapatnam","ans":"B","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Transport & Infrastructure","tags":["Shipbuilding","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"The source identifies Kochi as the correct answer.\nThe major shipbuilding facility associated with Kochi is Cochin Shipyard.\nIt is an important shipbuilding and ship-repair centre.","revisionPoints":"Cochin Shipyard → Kochi, Kerala.\nShipbuilding is an important component of India's maritime industrial infrastructure.\nKochi is an important port city on the Arabian Sea."},
{"no":4,"text":"Three flags, each of different colours, are available for a military exercise. Using these flags, different codes can be generated by waving:\n1. A single flag of different colours.\n2. Any two flags in a different sequence of colours.\n3. Three flags in a different sequence of colours.\nThe maximum number of codes that can be generated is:","A":"6","B":"9","C":"15","D":"18","ans":"C","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Quantitative Aptitude","tags":["UPSC","2003"],"expl":"For one flag:\n3 possibilities.\nFor two flags:\n\\(3P2 = 3 × 2 = 6\\).\nFor three flags:\n\\(3P3 = 3! = 6\\).\nTotal:\n\\(3 + 6 + 6 = 15\\).","revisionPoints":"Permutation is used when order matters.\n\\(nP r = \\frac{n!}{(n-r)!}\\).\nFor three distinct objects:\n\\(3P1 = 3\\)\n\\(3P2 = 6\\)\n\\(3P3 = 6\\)\nTotal = 15."},
{"no":5,"text":"Match the following organisations with their locations:\nOrganisation\nLocation\nA. National Sugar Institute\n1. Dehradun\nB. Mishra Dhatu Nigam Limited\n2. Kamptee\nC. Institute of Military Law\n3. Pune\nD. Institute of National Integration\n4. Hyderabad","A":"3, 1, 4, 2","B":"5, 1, 4, 3","C":"3, 4, 5, 2","D":"5, 4, 2, 3","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Institutions","tags":["National Institutes","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"The source provides (d) as the answer.\nThe matching requires identifying the locations of the respective institutions.\nThe question tests knowledge of specialised national institutions and their locations.","revisionPoints":"Match-the-following questions often test institutional geography.\nMishra Dhatu Nigam Limited → associated with specialised materials and metallurgy.\nInstitute-location questions should be memorised through repeated revision."},
{"no":6,"text":"Tim Montgomery set a new world record at the IAAF Grand Prix Final in 2002 in:","A":"Long Jump","B":"110 m Hurdle Race","C":"100 m Dash","D":"High Jump","ans":"C","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Sports","tags":["Athletics","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"Tim Montgomery was a prominent American sprinter.\nIn 2002, he set the then world record in the 100 metres.\nHis recorded time was 9.78 seconds.","revisionPoints":"Tim Montgomery → 100 m sprint.\nIAAF → International Association of Athletics Federations, now World Athletics.\nSports-record questions are time-sensitive and should be understood in their historical context."},
{"no":7,"text":"With reference to the work of the human kidney, consider the following statements:\n1. After waste is removed in the kidney, the cleaner blood is sent back through the renal artery.\n2. From Bowman’s capsule, the filtered liquid passes through tiny tubes where much of the glucose is reabsorbed and sent back to the blood in the renal vein.\nWhich of these statements is/are correct?","A":"Only 1","B":"Only 2","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biology","tags":["Human Kidney","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"Statement 1 — Incorrect:\nBlood enters the kidney through the renal artery.\nFiltered blood leaves through the renal vein.\nStatement 2 — Incorrect:\nGlucose is reabsorbed from the renal tubules into the blood.\nHowever, the statement's description of the process and vessels is not correct as framed.\nHence the source answer is (d).","revisionPoints":"Renal artery → carries blood to kidney.\nRenal vein → carries filtered blood away from kidney.\nNephron → functional unit of kidney.\nBowman’s capsule → receives filtrate from glomerulus.\nGlucose is normally reabsorbed in the renal tubules."},
{"no":8,"text":"A hydrogen-inflated polythene balloon is released from the surface of the Earth. As the balloon rises to a higher altitude in the atmosphere, it will:","A":"Decrease in size","B":"Flatten into a disc-like shape","C":"Increase in size","D":"Maintain the same size and shape","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Physics","tags":["Atmospheric Pressure","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"Atmospheric pressure decreases as altitude increases.\nThe gas inside the balloon therefore expands.\nSince the polythene balloon is flexible, its volume increases.\nHence the balloon increases in size.","revisionPoints":"Atmospheric pressure decreases with altitude.\nGas expands when external pressure decreases.\nBoyle's law: at constant temperature, P ∝ 1/V.\nHydrogen is less dense than air, producing buoyancy."},
{"no":9,"text":"The power to enlarge the jurisdiction of the Supreme Court of India with respect to any matter included in the Union List of Legislative Powers rests with:","A":"President of India","B":"Chief Justice of India","C":"Parliament","D":"Union Ministry of Law, Justice and Company Affairs","ans":"C","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Judiciary","tags":["Supreme Court","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"The Constitution permits Parliament to enlarge the jurisdiction of the Supreme Court in certain matters.\nThis is particularly relevant to matters in the Union List.\nTherefore, the power rests with Parliament.","revisionPoints":"Supreme Court jurisdiction is primarily constitutionally defined.\nParliament can expand the Court's jurisdiction through legislation in specified areas.\nUnion List → subjects on which Parliament has legislative competence.\nSupreme Court is the highest judicial authority in India."},
{"no":10,"text":"Which one of the following High Courts has territorial jurisdiction over the Andaman and Nicobar Islands?","A":"Andhra Pradesh","B":"Calcutta","C":"Madras","D":"Orissa","ans":"B","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Judiciary","tags":["High Courts","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"The Calcutta High Court has jurisdiction over the Andaman and Nicobar Islands.\nThis is an important example of a Union Territory falling under the jurisdiction of a High Court located in another part of India.","revisionPoints":"Andaman and Nicobar Islands → jurisdiction of Calcutta High Court.\nHigh Court jurisdiction is not always limited to the State in which the Court is physically located.\nUnion Territories may have jurisdiction arrangements through designated High Courts."},
{"no":11,"text":"Under which Article of the Indian Constitution did the President make a reference to the Supreme Court to seek the Court’s opinion on the constitutional validity of the Election Commission’s decision on deferring the Gujarat Assembly elections in 2002?","A":"Article 142","B":"Article 143","C":"Article 144","D":"Article 145","ans":"B","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Judiciary","tags":["Presidential Reference","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"Article 143 deals with the President's power to seek the advisory opinion of the Supreme Court.\nThe President may refer questions of law or fact of public importance to the Supreme Court.\nTherefore, the Gujarat election matter was referred under Article 143.","revisionPoints":"Article 143 → Presidential reference / advisory jurisdiction.\nArticle 142 → power to do complete justice.\nArticle 144 → authorities must act in aid of Supreme Court.\nArticle 145 → rules of Court."},
{"no":12,"text":"Consider the following statements:\n1. India launched its first full-fledged meteorological satellite, METSAT, in September 2002.\n2. For the first time, the space vehicle PSLV-C4 carried a payload of more than 1000 kg into a geosynchronous orbit.\nWhich of these statements is/are correct?","A":"Only 1","B":"Only 2","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Space","tags":["METSAT","PSLV","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"The source identifies both statements as correct.\nMETSAT was India's meteorological satellite initiative.\nPSLV-C4 was associated with the launch of METSAT into geosynchronous orbit.\nThe question highlights an important milestone in India's space programme.","revisionPoints":"METSAT → meteorological satellite.\nPSLV → Polar Satellite Launch Vehicle.\nMeteorological satellites help in weather observation and forecasting.\nSatellite technology has applications in communication, weather, remote sensing and disaster management."},
{"no":13,"text":"Nanda Devi peak forms a part of:","A":"Assam Himalayas","B":"Kumaon Himalayas","C":"Nepal Himalayas","D":"Punjab Himalayas","ans":"B","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography (India)","tags":["Himalayas","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"Nanda Devi is located in Uttarakhand.\nIt forms part of the Kumaon Himalayas.\nNanda Devi is one of India's prominent Himalayan peaks.","revisionPoints":"Nanda Devi → Uttarakhand.\nKumaon Himalayas → eastern part of the Uttarakhand Himalayas.\nNanda Devi is among India's highest peaks.\nHimalayan regional divisions are important for Indian geography."},
{"no":14,"text":"Consider the following statements:\n1. India is the original home of the cotton plant.\n2. India is the first country in the world to develop hybrid cotton varieties leading to increased production.\nWhich of these statements is/are correct?","A":"Only 1","B":"Only 2","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Agriculture & Allied","tags":["Cotton","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"The source identifies both statements as correct.\nCotton has a long history of cultivation in the Indian subcontinent.\nIndia also played an important role in the development and adoption of hybrid cotton.","revisionPoints":"Cotton is an important fibre crop.\nIndia has a long history of cotton cultivation and textile production.\nHybrid varieties can improve yield and other agricultural characteristics.\nCotton requires suitable temperature, moisture and soil conditions."},
{"no":15,"text":"Who is the author of the book ‘New Dimensions of India’s Foreign Policy’?","A":"A. B. Vajpayee","B":"Jaswant Singh","C":"P. C. Alexander","D":"Yashwant Sinha","ans":"A","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Books & Authors","tags":["Foreign Policy","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"The source identifies Atal Bihari Vajpayee as the author.\nVajpayee was an important figure in India's foreign policy and served as Prime Minister.\nThe book deals with India's foreign-policy perspectives.","revisionPoints":"A. B. Vajpayee → former Prime Minister of India.\nForeign policy questions can connect personalities with books, doctrines and diplomatic events.\nStatic book-author questions were common in older UPSC papers."},
{"no":16,"text":"How did the Mughal Emperor Jahandar Shah’s reign come to an early end?","A":"He was deposed by his wazir","B":"He died due to a slip while climbing down steps","C":"He was defeated by his nephew in a battle","D":"He died of sickness due to excessive consumption of wine","ans":"C","category":"History","subcategory":"Medieval India","tags":["Mughal Empire","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"Jahandar Shah became Mughal emperor after the death of Bahadur Shah I.\nHis reign was very short.\nHe was defeated by his nephew Farrukhsiyar.\nHe was subsequently captured and killed.","revisionPoints":"Jahandar Shah → Mughal emperor, 1712–1713.\nFarrukhsiyar succeeded after defeating Jahandar Shah.\nThe early 18th century saw increasing political instability in the Mughal Empire.\nMughal succession disputes contributed to imperial decline."},
{"no":17,"text":"In India, among the following locations, the Dutch established their earliest factory at:","A":"Surat","B":"Pulicat","C":"Cochin","D":"Cassimbazar","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Colonial History","tags":["Dutch East India Company","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"The Dutch established an early and important trading centre at Pulicat on the Coromandel Coast.\nPulicat became the headquarters of Dutch activities in India for a period.\nThe Dutch East India Company expanded its trade network along India's coastal regions.","revisionPoints":"Dutch East India Company → VOC.\nPulicat → important Dutch settlement on the Coromandel Coast.\nEuropean trading powers established coastal factories before political expansion.\nMajor European powers in India included Portuguese, Dutch, English and French."},
{"no":18,"text":"Consider the following statements:\n1. While members of the Rajya Sabha are associated with the Committees on Public Accounts and Public Undertakings, members of the Committee on Estimates are drawn entirely from Lok Sabha.\n2. The Ministry of Parliamentary Affairs works under the overall direction of the Cabinet Committee on Parliamentary Affairs.\n3. The Minister of Parliamentary Affairs nominates Members of Parliament on Committees, Councils, Boards and Commissions etc. set up by the Government of India in various ministries.\nWhich of these statements are correct?","A":"1 and 2","B":"2 and 3","C":"1 and 3","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"A","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Parliament","tags":["Parliamentary Committees","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"Statement 1 — Correct: The Estimates Committee consists entirely of members of the Lok Sabha.\nStatement 2 — Correct: The Ministry of Parliamentary Affairs functions under the overall direction of the Cabinet Committee on Parliamentary Affairs.\nStatement 3 — Incorrect according to the source.\nHence, 1 and 2 are correct.","revisionPoints":"Estimates Committee → only Lok Sabha members.\nPublic Accounts Committee → members from both Houses.\nCommittee on Public Undertakings → members from both Houses.\nParliamentary committees are important instruments of legislative oversight."},
{"no":19,"text":"The Genetic Engineering Approval Committee, whose permission was required for cultivation of genetically modified crops such as Bt Cotton in India, was under the Union Minister of:","A":"Agriculture","B":"Environment and Forests","C":"Commerce and Industry","D":"Rural Development","ans":"B","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Government Bodies","tags":["Genetic Engineering Approval Committee","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"The source places the Genetic Engineering Approval Committee under the Ministry of Environment and Forests.\nThe committee dealt with regulatory approval concerning genetically modified organisms and crops.\nBt Cotton is a genetically modified crop containing genes derived from Bacillus thuringiensis.","revisionPoints":"Bt Cotton → genetically modified cotton.\nBacillus thuringiensis → source of Bt toxin genes.\nBiosafety regulation is important for genetically modified organisms.\nEnvironment-related regulation can involve agriculture, biotechnology and public health considerations."},
{"no":20,"text":"As per Indian Protocol, who among the following ranks highest in the order of precedence?","A":"Deputy Prime Minister","B":"Former President","C":"Governor of a State within his State","D":"Speaker of Lok Sabha","ans":"C","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Warrant of Precedence","tags":["Warrant of Precedence","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"The Warrant of Precedence establishes ceremonial precedence.\nA Governor has a particularly high position within the State.\nThe source therefore identifies the Governor of a State, when within his/her State, as the highest among the options.","revisionPoints":"Warrant of Precedence deals with ceremonial/status arrangements.\nGovernor → constitutional head of a State.\nSpeaker → presiding officer of Lok Sabha.\nCeremonial precedence should not be confused with executive or legislative powers."},
{"no":21,"text":"Among the following cities, which one is at the highest altitude above mean sea level?","A":"Bangalore","B":"Delhi","C":"Jodhpur","D":"Nagpur","ans":"A","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography (India)","tags":["Indian Cities","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"Bangalore is located at a relatively high elevation in the Deccan Plateau.\nAmong the given cities, it has the highest altitude above mean sea level.\nIts elevated location contributes to its relatively moderate climate.","revisionPoints":"Bangalore → Deccan Plateau.\nDelhi → northern plains.\nJodhpur → Rajasthan.\nNagpur → central India.\nAltitude influences temperature and climate."},
{"no":22,"text":"Consider the following statements:\n1. Steam at 100°C and boiling water at 100°C contain the same amount of heat.\n2. Latent heat of fusion of ice is equal to the latent heat of vaporization of water.\n3. In an air-conditioned room, heat is extracted from the room air at the evaporator coils and rejected outside at the condenser coils.\nWhich of these statements is/are correct?","A":"1 and 2","B":"2 and 3","C":"Only 2","D":"Only 3","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Physics","tags":["Latent Heat","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"Statement 1 — Incorrect: Steam at 100°C contains additional latent heat compared with boiling water at 100°C.\nStatement 2 — Incorrect: Latent heat of vaporization is much greater than latent heat of fusion.\nStatement 3 — Correct: An air conditioner absorbs heat through the evaporator and rejects it through the condenser.\nTherefore, only Statement 3 is correct.","revisionPoints":"Latent heat → heat involved in change of state without temperature change.\nVaporization requires more latent heat than fusion.\nEvaporator → absorbs heat.\nCondenser → rejects heat.\nRefrigeration works through heat transfer rather than destruction of heat."},
{"no":23,"text":"Consider the following statements:\n1. Molasses is a by-product of the sugar production process.\n2. Bagasse obtained in sugar mills is used as fuel in boilers to generate steam for sugar factories.\n3. Sugar can only be produced from sugarcane as the raw material.\nWhich of these statements are correct?","A":"1 and 2","B":"2 and 3","C":"1 and 3","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Agriculture & Allied","tags":["Sugar Industry","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"Statement 1 — Correct: Molasses is obtained as a by-product during sugar production.\nStatement 2 — Correct: Bagasse is the fibrous residue left after crushing sugarcane and can be burned as fuel.\nStatement 3 — Incorrect: Sugar can also be produced from other crops such as sugar beet.\nHence, 1 and 2 are correct.","revisionPoints":"Molasses → by-product of sugar industry.\nBagasse → fibrous residue of sugarcane.\nBagasse can be used for cogeneration of heat and electricity.\nSugarcane and sugar beet are important sugar-producing crops."},
{"no":24,"text":"Consider the following statements:\n1. The longitude of Jabalpur’s location is between those of Indore and Bhopal.\n2. The latitude of Aurangabad’s location is between those of Vadodara and Pune.\n3. Bangalore is situated more southward than Chennai.\nWhich of these statements is/are correct?","A":"1 and 3","B":"Only 2","C":"2 and 3","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"C","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography (India)","tags":["Indian Cities","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"The source identifies Statements 2 and 3 as correct.\nStatement 1 does not satisfy the geographical longitude relationship given.\nAurangabad lies between Vadodara and Pune in terms of latitude.\nBangalore lies farther south than Chennai.","revisionPoints":"Latitude determines north-south position.\nLongitude determines east-west position.\nHigher latitude → farther north.\nLower latitude → farther south."},
{"no":25,"text":"Which one among the following States is smallest in area?","A":"Andhra Pradesh","B":"Gujarat","C":"Karnataka","D":"Tamil Nadu","ans":"D","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Indian States","tags":["Area of States","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"Among the four options, Tamil Nadu has the smallest geographical area.\nGujarat, Karnataka and Andhra Pradesh are geographically larger.","revisionPoints":"Tamil Nadu → southeastern India.\nGujarat → western India.\nKarnataka → southern India.\nAndhra Pradesh → southeastern India.\nState-area questions require comparison of geographical area, not population."},
{"no":26,"text":"A four-wheel vehicle moving on a sharp circular path at high speed will:\n1. Overturn about its outer wheels.\n2. Overturn about its inner wheels.\n3. Skid outwards.\n4. Skid inwards.\nWhich of these statements are correct?","A":"1 and 3","B":"2 and 4","C":"2 and 3","D":"1 and 4","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Physics","tags":["Circular Motion","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"During sharp turning at high speed, the vehicle experiences an outward tendency due to inertia.\nIt may skid outward if friction is insufficient.\nThe vehicle may also tend to overturn about its inner wheels.\nTherefore, statements 2 and 3 are correct.","revisionPoints":"Circular motion requires centripetal force.\nFriction between tyres and road provides the required force.\nExcessive speed increases the risk of skidding.\nVehicle stability depends on centre of gravity, speed, turning radius and road friction."},
{"no":27,"text":"The Basque people, frequently in the news for their separatist activity, live in:","A":"France","B":"Italy","C":"Portugal","D":"Spain","ans":"D","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Countries in News","tags":["Basque Separatism","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"The Basque region is located primarily in northern Spain, with part extending into southwestern France.\nThe Basque separatist movement was particularly associated with the organisation ETA.","revisionPoints":"Basque region → Spain and France.\nETA → historically associated with Basque separatism.\nSpain has several distinct regional identities.\nPolitical geography questions often involve ethnic and linguistic regions."},
{"no":28,"text":"Match the following high officials with their organisations:\nHigh Official\nOrganisation\nA. C. P. Jain\n1. Indian Airlines\nB. S. Ramadorai\n2. NTPC\nC. Sunil Arora\n3. TCS\nD. Vivek Paul\n4. Wipro Technologies","A":"4, 1, 3, 2","B":"4, 3, 1, 2","C":"2, 3, 1, 4","D":"2, 1, 3, 4","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Corporate Leadership","tags":["Notable Personalities","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"C. P. Jain → NTPC\nS. Ramadorai → TCS\nSunil Arora → Indian Airlines\nVivek Paul → Wipro Technologies\nTherefore: 2, 3, 1, 4.","revisionPoints":"S. Ramadorai → associated with TCS.\nVivek Paul → associated with Wipro.\nMatch-the-following questions can test contemporary corporate leadership.\nSuch questions are especially time-sensitive."},
{"no":29,"text":"Which one of the following statements is correct?","A":"Liquid sodium is employed as a coolant in nuclear reactors.","B":"Calcium carbonate is an ingredient of toothpaste.","C":"Bordeaux mixture consists of sodium sulphate and lime.","D":"Zinc amalgams are used as dental fillings.","ans":"A","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Chemistry","tags":["Nuclear Reactors","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"Liquid sodium has excellent heat-transfer properties and a high boiling point.\nIt is used as a coolant in certain fast breeder reactor systems.\nBordeaux mixture contains copper sulphate and lime, not sodium sulphate.\nDental amalgams traditionally involve mercury with metals such as silver and tin.","revisionPoints":"Liquid sodium → nuclear reactor coolant.\nBordeaux mixture → copper sulphate + lime.\nAmalgam → mercury combined with another metal/alloy.\nCalcium carbonate can also be used in some toothpaste formulations, making the wording of this historical question noteworthy."},
{"no":30,"text":"Consider the following statements:\n1. The Cholas defeated Pandya and Chera rulers and established their domination over peninsular India in early medieval times.\n2. The Cholas sent an expedition against the Sailendra Empire of Southeast Asia and conquered some areas.\nWhich of these statements is/are correct?","A":"Only 1","B":"Only 2","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"C","category":"History","subcategory":"Medieval India","tags":["Chola Empire","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"Statement 1 — Correct: Chola rulers expanded their power over large parts of southern India.\nStatement 2 — Correct: Rajendra Chola I conducted a famous naval expedition against the Srivijaya/Sailendra sphere in Southeast Asia.\nHence both statements are correct.","revisionPoints":"Chola power reached its peak under Rajaraja Chola I and Rajendra Chola I.\nRajendra Chola I → major naval expedition to Southeast Asia.\nCholas were known for strong naval capabilities.\nChola administration and temple architecture are important UPSC topics."},
{"no":31,"text":"Diffusion of light in the atmosphere takes place due to:","A":"Carbon dioxide","B":"Dust particles","C":"Helium","D":"Water vapours","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Physics","tags":["Light Diffusion","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"Atmospheric particles scatter incoming light.\nDust and other suspended particles contribute to the diffusion/scattering of light.\nThis phenomenon contributes to atmospheric visibility and appearance of the sky.","revisionPoints":"Scattering → redirection of light by particles.\nAtmospheric dust and aerosols influence light scattering.\nRayleigh scattering by molecules is responsible for the blue appearance of the sky.\nLarger particles can produce different scattering effects."},
{"no":32,"text":"In India, the first bank of limited liability managed by Indians and founded in 1881 was:","A":"Hindustan Commercial Bank","B":"Oudh Commercial Bank","C":"Punjab National Bank","D":"Punjab and Sind Bank","ans":"A","category":"History","subcategory":"Economic History","tags":["Indian Banking History","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"The source identifies Hindustan Commercial Bank as the answer.\nThe question concerns the early development of Indian-managed banking institutions.\nSuch institutions formed part of the evolution of modern banking in colonial India.","revisionPoints":"Banking history is an important part of modern Indian economic history.\nEarly Indian banks were established before independence.\nPunjab National Bank was founded in 1894.\nBank-related questions can involve chronology, nationalisation and institutional development."},
{"no":33,"text":"Which one of the following statements is correct?","A":"The Press Council of India is an autonomous quasi-judicial body established under an Act of Parliament.","B":"The Press Information Bureau provides accreditation to media persons so as to have easy access to information from government sources.","C":"Among all the States of India, Maharashtra publishes the largest number of newspapers.","D":"Press Trust of India is the largest news agency in the country.","ans":"C","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Media","tags":["Press Council of India","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"The source identifies Maharashtra as the correct answer.\nMaharashtra has historically had a large and diverse newspaper industry.\nThe question tests media-related facts from the period.","revisionPoints":"Press Council of India → statutory, quasi-judicial body.\nPIB → government communication and information dissemination.\nPTI → major Indian news agency.\nNewspaper circulation/publication statistics can change over time."},
{"no":34,"text":"The government holding in BPCL is:","A":"More than 70%","B":"Between 60% and 70%","C":"Between 50% and 60%","D":"Less than 50%","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Public Sector Undertakings","tags":["BPCL","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"This question refers to the government shareholding in Bharat Petroleum Corporation Limited (BPCL) at the time of the 2003 examination.\nThe source gives the holding as being between 60% and 70%.","revisionPoints":"BPCL → Bharat Petroleum Corporation Limited.\nIt is a major public-sector oil company.\nGovernment ownership and disinvestment have changed over time.\nFor historical UPSC questions, answer according to the relevant period."},
{"no":35,"text":"Consider the following statements regarding relations between India and Pakistan:\n1. During the Shimla Agreement, Indira Gandhi and Zulfikar Ali Bhutto agreed to maintain the sanctity of the Line of Control.\n2. Lahore Summit took place in 1997.\n3. Islamabad Summit was held between Rajiv Gandhi and Nawaz Sharif.\nWhich of these statements is/are correct?","A":"1, 2 and 3","B":"1 and 3","C":"Only 2","D":"Only 1","ans":"D","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"India's Foreign Relations","tags":["India-Pakistan Relations","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"Statement 1 — Correct: The Shimla Agreement of 1972 included commitments concerning the Line of Control.\nStatement 2 — Incorrect: The Lahore Summit took place in 1999.\nStatement 3 — Incorrect: The statement does not correctly identify the leaders associated with the Islamabad summit.\nTherefore, only Statement 1 is correct.","revisionPoints":"Shimla Agreement → 1972.\nIndira Gandhi and Zulfikar Ali Bhutto.\nLahore Declaration/Summit → 1999.\nIndia-Pakistan relations frequently appear through bilateral agreements and summits."},
{"no":36,"text":"During the year 2000–01, which industry recorded the highest growth rate in India?","A":"Cement","B":"Coal","C":"Electricity","D":"Steel","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Industry","tags":["Industrial Growth","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"According to the source's 2000–01 data, the steel industry recorded the highest growth rate among the options.\nThis is a period-specific economic statistic.","revisionPoints":"Industrial growth can be measured through production/output growth.\nSteel is a core/basic industry.\nCore industries have strong linkages with infrastructure and manufacturing.\nHistorical economic statistics should be interpreted with the relevant year."},
{"no":37,"text":"An oil tanker is partially filled with oil and moves forward on a level road with uniform acceleration. The free surface of oil will:","A":"Remain horizontal","B":"Be inclined to the horizontal with smaller depth at the rear end","C":"Be inclined to the horizontal with larger depth at the rear end","D":"Assume a parabolic curve","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Physics","tags":["Fluid Dynamics","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"When the tanker accelerates forward, the oil experiences an apparent force towards the rear.\nThe free surface tilts.\nTherefore, the oil level becomes higher at the rear and lower at the front.\nHence the free surface is inclined to the horizontal.","revisionPoints":"Accelerating liquid → free surface tilts.\nDecelerating liquid → opposite inclination.\nFree surface of a liquid under uniform acceleration is generally a plane.\nPressure distribution changes with the effective force acting on the liquid."},
{"no":38,"text":"In which one of the following countries is the rupee its currency?","A":"Bhutan","B":"Malaysia","C":"Maldives","D":"Seychelles","ans":"D","category":"Geography","subcategory":"World Geography","tags":["World Currencies","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"The source identifies Seychelles.\nThe currency of Seychelles is the Seychellois rupee.\nIndia uses the Indian rupee, while Bhutan uses the ngultrum and Maldives uses the rufiyaa.","revisionPoints":"Seychelles → Seychellois rupee.\nBhutan → Ngultrum.\nMaldives → Rufiyaa.\nCurrency questions can test similarities in currency names across countries."},
{"no":39,"text":"Which one of the following countries is landlocked?","A":"Bolivia","B":"Peru","C":"Suriname","D":"Uruguay","ans":"A","category":"Geography","subcategory":"World Geography","tags":["Landlocked Countries","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"Bolivia is a landlocked country in South America.\nPeru, Suriname and Uruguay have coastlines.\nBolivia lost its Pacific coastline following the War of the Pacific.","revisionPoints":"Bolivia → landlocked South American country.\nPeru → Pacific coast.\nSuriname → Atlantic coast.\nUruguay → Atlantic coast.\nLandlocked countries have no direct access to an ocean or sea."},
{"no":40,"text":"In December 2002, the Prime Minister of India signed the ‘Delhi Declaration’ with the:","A":"President of Indonesia","B":"President of Russia","C":"Prime Minister of Cambodia","D":"Prime Minister of Laos","ans":"B","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"India's Foreign Relations","tags":["India-Russia Relations","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"The source identifies the President of Russia.\nThe Delhi Declaration was associated with India-Russia relations.\nIt reflected the continuing strategic and diplomatic relationship between the two countries.","revisionPoints":"India-Russia relations have a long strategic history.\nBilateral declarations often cover defence, trade, technology and diplomatic cooperation.\nContemporary-affairs questions in older UPSC papers are strongly linked to events of the examination year."},
{"no":41,"text":"Among the following cities, which one is nearest to the Tropic of Cancer?","A":"Delhi","B":"Kolkata","C":"Jodhpur","D":"Nagpur","ans":"B","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography (India)","tags":["Tropic of Cancer","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"The Tropic of Cancer lies at approximately 23½° North latitude.\nKolkata is located at about 22.6° N, making it the closest among the given cities.\nJodhpur and Delhi are considerably farther north.\nNagpur is south of the Tropic of Cancer.","revisionPoints":"Tropic of Cancer: 23½° N.\nIt passes through 8 Indian states.\nIt is an important reference latitude for Indian geography.\nKolkata is geographically close to the Tropic of Cancer."},
{"no":42,"text":"‘Mrichchhakatika’, an ancient Indian book written by Shudraka, deals with:","A":"The love affair of a rich merchant with the daughter of a courtesan","B":"The victory of Chandragupta II over the Shaka Kshatrapas of western India","C":"The military expeditions and exploits of Samudragupta","D":"The love affair between a Gupta King and a princess of Kamarupa","ans":"A","category":"History","subcategory":"Ancient India","tags":["Mrichchhakatika","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"Mrichchhakatika means “The Little Clay Cart.”\nIt is a Sanskrit drama traditionally attributed to Shudraka.\nIts central story involves Charudatta, a wealthy merchant, and Vasantasena, a courtesan.\nThe work combines romance, politics and social life.","revisionPoints":"Author: Shudraka.\nLanguage: Sanskrit.\nGenre: Drama.\nImportant characters: Charudatta and Vasantasena.\nTitle meaning: The Little Clay Cart."},
{"no":43,"text":"Consider the following statements:\n1. Vardhamana Mahavira’s mother was the daughter of Lichchhavi chief Chetaka.\n2. Gautama Buddha’s mother was a princess from the Koshalan dynasty.\n3. Parshvanatha, the 23rd Tirthankara, belonged to Banaras.\nWhich of these statements is/are correct?","A":"Only 1","B":"Only 2","C":"2 and 3","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"C","category":"History","subcategory":"Ancient India","tags":["Jainism","Buddhism","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"The source marks 2 and 3 as correct.\nParshvanatha is traditionally associated with Banaras/Varanasi.\nGautama Buddha's mother was Maya, associated with the Koliya lineage; therefore, this question's historical framing is problematic.\nThe source answer should be retained for source-based preparation.","revisionPoints":"Mahavira: 24th Tirthankara.\nParshvanatha: 23rd Tirthankara.\nMahavira was associated with the Jñatrika clan.\nBuddha's mother: Maya (Mahamaya).\nParshvanatha is traditionally associated with Varanasi."},
{"no":44,"text":"A car travels the first one-third of a certain distance at 10 km/hr, the next one-third at 20 km/hr, and the last one-third at 60 km/hr. The average speed for the whole journey is:","A":"18 km/hr","B":"24 km/hr","C":"30 km/hr","D":"36 km/hr","ans":"A","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Quantitative Aptitude","tags":["UPSC","2003"],"expl":"Let total distance = 3D.\nTime taken:\nFirst third = D/10\nSecond third = D/20\nThird third = D/60\nTotal time = D(1/10 + 1/20 + 1/60)\n= D(6 + 3 + 1)/60 = D/6\nAverage speed = Total distance / Total time\n= 3D ÷ D/6 = 18 km/hr.","revisionPoints":"For equal distances, average speed is harmonic mean, not arithmetic mean.\nFormula:Average speed = Total distance / Total time.\nDo not use (10 + 20 + 60)/3 for this type of question."},
{"no":45,"text":"The purpose of adding sodium sulphate and sodium silicate to detergent washing powder is:\n1. To keep the washing powder dry.\n2. To maintain the alkalinity of the powder.\nWhich of these statements is/are correct?","A":"Only 1","B":"Only 2","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"A","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Chemistry","tags":["Detergents","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"The source identifies keeping the washing powder dry as the correct purpose.\nSodium sulphate is commonly used as a filler and helps improve the physical properties of detergent powder.\nSodium silicate is associated with builder/alkalinity functions, making the source key somewhat questionable from a chemistry perspective.\nFor this source-based question, remember the marked answer: Only 1.","revisionPoints":"Detergents commonly contain:\nSurfactants\nBuilders\nFillers\nWater softeners\nSodium silicate can function as a builder and alkaline component.\nSodium sulphate is commonly used as a filler."},
{"no":46,"text":"Debenture holders of a company are its:","A":"Shareholders","B":"Creditors","C":"Debtors","D":"Directors","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Corporate Finance","tags":["Debentures","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"A debenture represents borrowed money/debt of a company.\nA debenture holder lends money to the company.\nTherefore, the company has a debt obligation towards the debenture holder.\nHence, debenture holders are creditors.","revisionPoints":"Shareholders → Owners of the company.\nDebenture holders → Creditors.\nDebenture holders generally receive interest.\nShareholders generally receive dividend, which is not a fixed debt obligation."},
{"no":47,"text":"Which one of the following statements is correct?","A":"The National Song Vande Mataram was composed by Bankimchandra Chatterji originally in Bengali.","B":"The National Calendar of India based on Saka era has its 1st Chaitra on 22nd March normally and 21st March in a leap year.","C":"The design of the National Flag of India was adopted by the Constituent Assembly on 22nd July 1947.","D":"Jana Gana Mana, composed originally in Bengali by Rabindranath Tagore, was adopted in its Hindi version by the Constituent Assembly on 24th January 1950 as the National Anthem.","ans":"A","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"National Symbols","tags":["National Symbols","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"Vande Mataram was composed by Bankimchandra Chatterji.\nIt was written in the context of the nationalist movement.\nIt appeared in his novel Anandamath.\nThe source marks option (a) as the answer.","revisionPoints":"National Song: Vande Mataram.\nComposer: Bankimchandra Chatterji.\nNational Anthem: Jana Gana Mana.\nComposer of National Anthem: Rabindranath Tagore.\nIndian National Flag was adopted on 22 July 1947."},
{"no":48,"text":"Which Article of the Indian Constitution provides that:\n“It shall be the duty of the Union to protect every State against external aggression and internal disturbance”?","A":"Article 215","B":"Article 275","C":"Article 325","D":"Article 355","ans":"D","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Constitution","tags":["Article 355","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"Article 355 imposes a duty on the Union to protect States.\nProtection is against:\nExternal aggression\nInternal disturbance","revisionPoints":"Article 355: Union's duty to protect States.\nArticle 356: Failure of constitutional machinery in a State.\nArticle 365: Failure to comply with Union directions.\nArticles 355 and 356 are important for Centre–State relations."},
{"no":49,"text":"A walks around a circular field at 1 round/hour, while B runs around it at 6 rounds/hour. They start in the same direction from the same point at 7:30 a.m. They shall first cross each other at:","A":"8:30 a.m.","B":"8:10 a.m.","C":"7:48 a.m.","D":"7:42 a.m.","ans":"D","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Quantitative Aptitude","tags":["UPSC","2003"],"expl":"Relative speed = 6 − 1 = 5 rounds/hour.\nTo overtake A, B must gain 1 complete round.\nTime = 1/5 hour.\n1/5 hour = 12 minutes.\nStarting at 7:30 a.m.:\n7:30 + 12 minutes = 7:42 a.m.","revisionPoints":"Same direction → difference of speeds.\nOpposite direction → sum of speeds.\nCircular track problems use relative motion.\nOne complete relative round corresponds to one overtaking."},
{"no":50,"text":"In India, stamp duties on financial transactions are:\n1. Levied and collected by the State Government.\n2. Appropriated by the Union Government.\nWhich of these statements is/are correct?","A":"Only 1","B":"Only 2","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"D","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Public Finance","tags":["Stamp Duty","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"The source marks both statements as incorrect.\nThe constitutional arrangement regarding stamp duties distinguishes between:\nLevy\nCollection\nAppropriation\nTherefore, the statements cannot simply be treated as “State levies and collects” and “Union appropriates.”","revisionPoints":"Stamp duties have a constitutional distribution mechanism.\nImportant constitutional provisions:\nArticle 268\nSeventh Schedule\nUPSC often tests the distinction between levied, collected, and appropriated."},
{"no":51,"text":"Which one of the following statements is correct?","A":"East Timor was a British colony for four centuries.","B":"European colonial rulers handed over East Timor to Indonesia in 1975.","C":"The United Nations took over East Timor in 1999 to prepare it for independence.","D":"East Timor finally declared its independence in December 2001.","ans":"C","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Countries in News","tags":["East Timor","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"East Timor was under Indonesian occupation before the UN transitional administration.\nFollowing the 1999 referendum and violence, the UN established a transitional administration.\nEast Timor eventually became independent on 20 May 2002.\nTherefore, option (d) is incorrect.","revisionPoints":"Present name: Timor-Leste.\nCapital: Dili.\nUN transitional administration began in 1999.\nIndependence: 20 May 2002.\nImportant example of UN transitional administration."},
{"no":52,"text":"Match the following Indian Constitutional provisions with the country from which they were derived:\nList I\nList II\nA. Directive Principles of State Policy\n1. Australia\nB. Fundamental Rights\n2. Canada\nC. Concurrent List\n3. Ireland\nD. India as a Union of States with greater powers to the Union\n4. United Kingdom\n5. USA\nCodes:","A":"5 4 1 2","B":"3 5 2 1","C":"5 4 2 1","D":"3 5 1 2","ans":"D","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Constitution","tags":["Sources of the Constitution","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"DPSP → Ireland\nFundamental Rights → USA\nConcurrent List → Australia\nStrong Centre / Union of States → Canada","revisionPoints":"Ireland → Directive Principles.\nUSA → Fundamental Rights, judicial review.\nAustralia → Concurrent List.\nCanada → Federation with a strong Centre.\nUK → Parliamentary system, rule of law, legislative procedures."},
{"no":53,"text":"With reference to Indian defence, which one of the following statements is correct?","A":"With the induction of Prithvi II, the IAF is the only air force in the world with surface-to-surface ballistic missiles under its command.","B":"Sukhoi-30 MKI jet fighters can launch air-to-air and air-to-surface precision missiles.","C":"Trishul is a supersonic surface-to-air missile with a range of 30 km.","D":"The indigenously built INS Prabal can launch surface-to-surface missiles.","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Defence Technology","tags":["Indian Defence","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"INS Prabal is a missile corvette of the Indian Navy.\nIt was equipped with anti-ship missiles capable of surface-to-surface engagement.\nThe source identifies option (d) as correct.","revisionPoints":"Prithvi: surface-to-surface missile family.\nTrishul: short-range surface-to-air missile system.\nSukhoi-30MKI: multirole fighter aircraft.\nINS Prabal: Indian Navy missile corvette."},
{"no":54,"text":"The Indira Gandhi Peace Prize, 2002 was awarded to:","A":"John Hume","B":"Nelson Mandela","C":"Norodom Sihanouk","D":"Sadako Ogata","ans":"C","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Awards","tags":["Indira Gandhi Peace Prize","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"The Indira Gandhi Prize for Peace, Disarmament and Development is an international award.\nThe source identifies Norodom Sihanouk, former King of Cambodia, as the 2002 recipient.","revisionPoints":"Award named after Indira Gandhi.\nAssociated with:\nPeace\nDisarmament\nDevelopment\nNorodom Sihanouk → 2002 according to the source."},
{"no":55,"text":"Match the following persons with their associated fields:\nPerson\nAssociated with\nA. Brijtnoham Lall Mungal\n1. Biotechnology\nB. Kiran Karnik\n2. Automobile Industry\nC. Kiran Mazumdar-Shaw\n3. Software Industry\nD. Sunil Mittal\n4. Telecom Industry\n5. Film Industry\nCodes:","A":"4 1 5 2","B":"4 3 1 2","C":"2 3 1 4","D":"2 1 5 4","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Business Personalities","tags":["Notable Personalities","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"Brijtnoham Lall Mungal → Automobile industry\nKiran Karnik → Software industry\nKiran Mazumdar-Shaw → Biotechnology\nSunil Mittal → Telecom industry","revisionPoints":"Kiran Mazumdar-Shaw → Biocon → Biotechnology\nKiran Karnik → NASSCOM / IT industry\nSunil Mittal → Telecom / Bharti\nMatch-the-following questions often combine business personalities and sectors."},
{"no":56,"text":"Whom did Mahesh Bhupathi and Max Mirnyi beat in the 2002 US Open men's doubles championship to win the title?","A":"Wayne Black and Kevin Ullyett","B":"Mike Bryan and Bob Bryan","C":"Jonas Bjorkman and Todd Woodbridge","D":"Jiri Novak and Radek Stepanek","ans":"D","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Sports","tags":["Tennis","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"Mahesh Bhupathi of India partnered with Max Mirnyi of Belarus.\nThey won the 2002 US Open men's doubles title.\nThe source identifies Jiri Novak and Radek Stepanek as the opponents in the final.","revisionPoints":"Mahesh Bhupathi → Tennis.\nMax Mirnyi → Belarusian tennis player.\n2002 US Open men's doubles → Bhupathi–Mirnyi.\nSports questions in older Prelims often tested recent international events."},
{"no":57,"text":"Four candles can last for 5, 4, 3 and 2 hours respectively. They are lit simultaneously and allowed to burn until three candles get extinguished. The cost of burning each candle is 75 paise per hour. The cost involved is:","A":"₹2.75","B":"₹3.75","C":"₹9.75","D":"₹12.50","ans":"C","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Quantitative Aptitude","tags":["UPSC","2003"],"expl":"The source key marks ₹9.75.\nHowever, the wording creates a calculation inconsistency.\nIf the candles burn simultaneously until the third candle goes out, that occurs after 4 hours:\n5-hour candle → 4 hours\n4-hour candle → 4 hours\n3-hour candle → 3 hours\n2-hour candle → 2 hours\nTotal candle-hours = 13 hours.\nAt ₹0.75/hour → ₹9.75.","revisionPoints":"Candle problems require calculating actual burning time of each candle.\nTotal cost = sum of candle-hours × cost per hour.\nHere: 4 + 4 + 3 + 2 = 13 candle-hours.\n13 × ₹0.75 = ₹9.75."},
{"no":58,"text":"Which one of the following statements is correct with reference to FEMA in India?","A":"FERA was replaced by FEMA in 2001.","B":"FERA had a sunset clause till 31 May 2002.","C":"Under FEMA, violation of foreign exchange rules has ceased to be a criminal offence.","D":"Enforcement Directorate can arrest and prosecute people for violation of foreign exchange rules under the new dispensation.","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"External Sector","tags":["FEMA","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"FERA was replaced by FEMA.\nFEMA came into force on 1 June 2000, not 2001.\nFEMA changed the approach from regulation/control toward management of foreign exchange.\nContraventions under FEMA are generally treated as civil offences, unlike the criminal framework under FERA.","revisionPoints":"FERA → Foreign Exchange Regulation Act.\nFEMA → Foreign Exchange Management Act, 1999.\nFEMA came into force: 1 June 2000.\nFERA was comparatively restrictive/control-oriented.\nFEMA is comparatively management-oriented."},
{"no":59,"text":"Consider the following statements:\n1. India's import of crude and petroleum products during 2001–02 accounted for about 27% of India's total imports.\n2. During 2001–02, India's exports increased by 10% compared with the previous year.\nWhich of these statements is/are correct?","A":"Only 1","B":"Only 2","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"External Sector","tags":["India's Trade","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"According to the source, statement 1 is correct.\nStatement 2 is incorrect because India's exports did not increase by 10% during the stated period.\nTherefore, only statement 1 is correct.","revisionPoints":"Petroleum imports form a major component of India's import bill.\nUPSC frequently asks about:\nImport composition\nExport growth\nTrade balance\nCurrent account\nAlways distinguish percentage share of imports from year-on-year growth."},
{"no":60,"text":"Who among the following was selected for the 2001 Dada Saheb Phalke Award?","A":"Dev Anand","B":"Lata Mangeshkar","C":"Ramanand Sagar","D":"Yash Chopra","ans":"D","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Awards","tags":["Dada Saheb Phalke Award","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"The Dadasaheb Phalke Award is India's highest award in the field of cinema.\nYash Chopra was the recipient for the year 2001.\nHe was a renowned Hindi film director and producer.","revisionPoints":"Dadasaheb Phalke Award → highest Indian cinema honour.\nNamed after Dadasaheb Phalke, pioneer of Indian cinema.\n2001 recipient → Yash Chopra.\nAward is presented for lifetime contribution to Indian cinema."},
{"no":61,"text":"Which one of the following statements is correct?","A":"Milk contains none of the B-vitamins","B":"Vitamin A (retinal) deficiency leads to dry and scaly skin","C":"One of the symptoms of scurvy is pain in the joints","D":"Vitamin B1 (thiamine) deficiency can lead to heart failure","ans":"A","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biology","tags":["Vitamins","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"The source marks (a) as the answer.\nMilk does contain several B-group vitamins, so this source answer is questionable from a scientific perspective.\nVitamin A deficiency is primarily associated with night blindness and xerophthalmia.\nVitamin B1 deficiency can cause beriberi, including cardiovascular manifestations.","revisionPoints":"Vitamin A → vision, epithelial tissues.\nVitamin B1 → thiamine.\nThiamine deficiency → beriberi.\nVitamin C deficiency → scurvy.\nMilk contains several vitamins and minerals."},
{"no":62,"text":"As per the Human Development Index given by UNDP, which sequence of South Asian countries is correct in the order of higher to lower development?","A":"India — Sri Lanka — Pakistan — Maldives","B":"Maldives — Sri Lanka — India — Pakistan","C":"Sri Lanka — Maldives — India — Pakistan","D":"Maldives — India — Pakistan — Sri Lanka","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Human Development","tags":["Human Development Index","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"The question refers to the UNDP Human Development Index rankings applicable at the time of the examination.\nThe source gives the sequence:\nMaldives\nSri Lanka\nIndia\nPakistan","revisionPoints":"HDI is published by UNDP.\nMajor dimensions:\nHealth\nEducation\nStandard of living\nHDI is a composite development indicator.\nRankings can change between editions."},
{"no":63,"text":"Which one of the following statements is correct?","A":"The velocity of sound in air increases with the increase of temperature.","B":"The velocity of sound in air is independent of pressure.","C":"The velocity of sound in air decreases as humidity increases.","D":"The velocity of sound in air is not affected by change in amplitude and frequency.","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Physics","tags":["Speed of Sound","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"The source marks (c).\nHowever, this is a source-key issue: scientifically, the speed of sound in air generally increases with humidity.\nSpeed of sound also increases with temperature.\nAmplitude and frequency do not determine the speed of sound in a given medium under fixed conditions.","revisionPoints":"Sound is a mechanical wave.\nSpeed of sound in air increases with temperature.\nHigher humidity generally increases the speed of sound.\nFrequency and amplitude do not normally determine wave speed in a given medium."},
{"no":64,"text":"Consider the following statements:\n1. India ranks first in the world in fruit production.\n2. India ranks second in the world in the export of tobacco.\nWhich of these statements is/are correct?","A":"Only 1","B":"Only 2","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Agriculture & Allied","tags":["Fruit Production","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"According to the source, India ranked first in fruit production.\nThe source considers the statement regarding second position in tobacco exports incorrect.\nHence, only statement 1 is correct.","revisionPoints":"India has historically been a major fruit-producing country.\nImportant fruit crops include:\nMango\nBanana\nCitrus\nGuava\nApple\nAgricultural rankings are often time-sensitive."},
{"no":65,"text":"Which one of the following statements is correct?","A":"Alliance Air is a wholly owned subsidiary of Indian Airlines.","B":"The Airports Authority of India manages seven of the country's international airports.","C":"The Airports Authority of India is the regulatory organisation for enforcing civil air regulations in India.","D":"It is the function of the Directorate General of Civil Aviation to plan and construct runways and terminal buildings and provide air safety services.","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Aviation","tags":["Alliance Air","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"The source identifies (a) as correct.\nThe question distinguishes between:\nAirport management/infrastructure functions.\nCivil aviation regulation.\nThe DGCA is associated with civil aviation regulation, while airport infrastructure functions are associated with the airport authorities.","revisionPoints":"DGCA → Civil aviation regulatory functions.\nAirport authorities → airport infrastructure and operations.\nAlliance Air was associated with Indian Airlines in the period referred to by the question.\nInstitutional roles are important in governance questions."},
{"no":66,"text":"Which one of the following Bills must be passed by each House of the Indian Parliament separately by special majority?","A":"Ordinary Bill","B":"Money Bill","C":"Finance Bill","D":"Constitution Amendment Bill","ans":"D","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Parliament","tags":["Constitution Amendment Bill","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"Constitutional amendments under Article 368 generally require a special majority.\nAn Ordinary Bill generally requires a simple majority.\nA Money Bill follows the special procedure under the Constitution.\nCertain constitutional amendments also require ratification by at least half of the States.","revisionPoints":"Article 368 → Constitutional amendment.\nSpecial majority generally means:\nMajority of total membership of the House, and\nTwo-thirds of members present and voting.\nSome amendments additionally require State ratification.\nMoney Bills have special constitutional procedures."},
{"no":67,"text":"Consider the following animals of India:\n1. Crocodile\n2. Elephant\nWhich of these is/are endangered species?","A":"Only 1","B":"Only 2","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"C","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["Endangered Species","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"The source identifies both crocodile and elephant as endangered species.\nBoth have historically faced major threats from habitat loss and human activities.\nConservation status can vary by species and classification system.","revisionPoints":"Major threats to wildlife:\nHabitat destruction\nPoaching\nHuman–wildlife conflict\nPollution\nIndian elephant is an important protected species.\nMugger, gharial and saltwater crocodile are major crocodilian species associated with India.\nConservation status should be checked with the relevant classification system."},
{"no":68,"text":"Which one among the following covers the highest percentage of forest area in the world?","A":"Temperate Coniferous forests","B":"Temperate Deciduous forests","C":"Tropical Monsoon forests","D":"Tropical Rain forests","ans":"A","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Forestry","tags":["Forest Types","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"The source identifies temperate coniferous forests as the category covering the highest percentage among the given options.\nConiferous forests are particularly extensive across the high northern latitudes.\nThey form a major component of the world's forest cover.","revisionPoints":"Coniferous forests → predominantly needle-leaved trees.\nMajor distribution → Canada, Alaska, Russia and northern Eurasia.\nTropical rainforests → extremely high biodiversity.\nForest type depends strongly on temperature and precipitation."},
{"no":69,"text":"A two-member committee consisting of one male and one female is to be constituted out of 5 males and 3 females.\nAmong the females, Ms. A refuses to be a member of the committee in which Mr. B is taken as the member.\nIn how many different ways can the committee be constituted?","A":"11","B":"12","C":"13","D":"14","ans":"D","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Quantitative Aptitude","tags":["UPSC","2003"],"expl":"Total possible committees:\nChoose 1 male from 5 = 5\nChoose 1 female from 3 = 3\nTotal = 5 × 3 = 15\nThe only prohibited combination is:\nMr. B + Ms. A\nTherefore:\n15 − 1 = 14","revisionPoints":"Fundamental counting principle:\nIf one choice has m possibilities and another has n, total = m × n.\nRestriction problems are solved by:\nTotal possibilities − prohibited possibilities.\nHere: 15 − 1 = 14."},
{"no":70,"text":"Consider the following statements:\n1. The last Mauryan ruler, Brihadratha, was assassinated by his commander-in-chief, Pushyamitra Sunga.\n2. The last Sunga king, Devabhuti, was assassinated by his Brahmana minister Vasudeva Kanva, who usurped the throne.\n3. The last ruler of the Kanva dynasty was deposed by the Andhras.\nWhich of these statements is/are correct?","A":"1 and 2","B":"Only 2","C":"Only 3","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"History","subcategory":"Ancient India","tags":["Sunga Dynasty","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"Brihadratha was the last Mauryan ruler.\nHe was killed by his commander Pushyamitra, who established the Sunga dynasty.\nDevabhuti was the last Sunga ruler.\nVasudeva Kanva overthrew the Sunga dynasty.\nThe Kanva dynasty was subsequently displaced by the Andhras/Satavahanas.","revisionPoints":"Maurya → Sunga → Kanva → Satavahana sequence.\nLast Mauryan ruler → Brihadratha.\nFounder of Sunga dynasty → Pushyamitra Sunga.\nFounder of Kanva dynasty → Vasudeva Kanva.\nDynastic succession is frequently tested in Ancient History."},
{"no":71,"text":"The aim of education as stated by Wood's Despatch of 1854 was:","A":"Creation of employment opportunities for native Indians","B":"Spread of Western culture in India","C":"Promotion of literacy among people using English as the medium","D":"Introduction of scientific research and rationalism in traditional Indian education","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"British Administration","tags":["Wood's Despatch","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"The source identifies (b) as the answer.\nWood's Despatch of 1854 was a major landmark in British educational policy in India.\nIt advocated an organised educational system and expansion of Western-style education.","revisionPoints":"Wood's Despatch → 1854.\nOften called the “Magna Carta of English Education in India.”\nAssociated with expansion of:\nEducation departments\nUniversities\nTeacher training\nWestern education\nImportant in the history of colonial education."},
{"no":72,"text":"Who won the title in the 2002 French Open men's singles tennis championship?","A":"Albert Costa","B":"Juan Carlos Ferrero","C":"Andre Agassi","D":"Pete Sampras","ans":"A","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Sports","tags":["French Open","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"Albert Costa of Spain won the 2002 French Open men's singles title.\nHe defeated fellow Spaniard Juan Carlos Ferrero in the final.","revisionPoints":"Tournament: French Open / Roland-Garros.\nSurface: Clay.\n2002 men's singles champion → Albert Costa.\nSpain has historically been strongly associated with clay-court tennis."},
{"no":73,"text":"Consider the following functions of the Finance Commission:\n1. To allow withdrawal of money from the Consolidated Fund of India.\n2. To allocate between the States the shares of proceeds of taxes.\n3. To consider applications for grants-in-aid from States.\n4. To supervise and report on whether Union and State governments are levying taxes according to budgetary provisions.\nWhich of these is/are correct?","A":"Only 1","B":"2 and 3","C":"3 and 4","D":"1, 2 and 4","ans":"B","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Finance Commission","tags":["Finance Commission","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"The Finance Commission is a constitutional body under Article 280.\nIt recommends:\nDistribution of tax proceeds between Union and States.\nAllocation of States' shares.\nPrinciples governing grants-in-aid.\nIt does not authorise withdrawal from the Consolidated Fund.\nIt does not supervise tax collection in the manner described in statements 1 and 4.","revisionPoints":"Article 280 → Finance Commission.\nConstituted by the President.\nMajor role → Centre–State fiscal relations.\nRecommends:\nTax devolution\nGrants-in-aid\nDistribution among States\nFinance Commission is different from the Planning Commission."},
{"no":74,"text":"Which one among the following States had the highest female literacy rate as per Census 2001?","A":"Chhattisgarh","B":"Madhya Pradesh","C":"Orissa","D":"Rajasthan","ans":"A","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Census","tags":["Census 2001","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"According to the source's Census 2001 data, Chhattisgarh had the highest female literacy rate among the given options.\nThe question is specifically tied to 2001 Census data.","revisionPoints":"Census 2001 is an important historical demographic dataset.\nLiteracy data can be analysed by:\nOverall literacy\nMale literacy\nFemale literacy\nGender gap in literacy is an important development indicator.\nAlways pay attention to the census year."},
{"no":75,"text":"Which one among the following countries had the lowest GDP per capita?","A":"China","B":"India","C":"Indonesia","D":"Sri Lanka","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Comparative Economics","tags":["GDP per Capita","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"The question refers to the economic data available at the time of the examination.\nAccording to the source, India had the lowest GDP per capita among the listed countries.","revisionPoints":"GDP per capita = GDP ÷ population.\nIt is an average measure of economic output per person.\nGDP per capita does not by itself measure:\nIncome distribution\nPoverty\nQuality of life\nHistorical rankings should be interpreted according to the year and dataset."},
{"no":76,"text":"Consider the following statements:\n1. In Macedonia, ethnic Albanians are a minority.\n2. In Kosovo, Serbians are a majority.\nWhich of these statements is/are correct?","A":"Only 1","B":"Only 2","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"C","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Countries in News","tags":["Balkans","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"The source marks both statements as correct.\nStatement 1 reflects the demographic position of ethnic Albanians in Macedonia/North Macedonia.\nStatement 2 is problematic: Serbs were not the majority population of Kosovo.\nTherefore, this is another historical/current-affairs source-key issue.","revisionPoints":"Macedonia is now officially called North Macedonia.\nKosovo has historically had a large ethnic Albanian majority.\nThe Balkans have experienced major ethnic and political conflicts.\nKosovo's status has been a significant issue in international relations."},
{"no":77,"text":"Israel has common borders with:","A":"Lebanon, Syria, Jordan and Egypt","B":"Lebanon, Syria, Turkey and Jordan","C":"Cyprus, Turkey, Jordan and Egypt","D":"Turkey, Syria, Iraq and Yemen","ans":"A","category":"Geography","subcategory":"World Geography","tags":["Israel","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"Israel has land borders with:\nLebanon\nSyria\nJordan\nEgypt\nThe Mediterranean Sea lies to its west.","revisionPoints":"Israel → eastern Mediterranean region.\nLand neighbours:\nLebanon\nSyria\nJordan\nEgypt\nMediterranean Sea → west.\nGeography questions often test neighbouring countries and boundaries."},
{"no":78,"text":"What is the correct sequence of the rivers Godavari, Mahanadi, Narmada and Tapi in descending order of their lengths?","A":"Godavari — Mahanadi — Narmada — Tapi","B":"Godavari — Narmada — Mahanadi — Tapi","C":"Narmada — Godavari — Tapi — Mahanadi","D":"Narmada — Tapi — Godavari — Mahanadi","ans":"B","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography (India)","tags":["Indian Rivers","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"Among these rivers:\nGodavari is the longest.\nNarmada is longer than Mahanadi.\nMahanadi is longer than Tapi.\nTherefore, descending order is:Godavari → Narmada → Mahanadi → Tapi.","revisionPoints":"Godavari → longest peninsular river.\nNarmada and Tapi flow generally westward.\nMahanadi flows eastward into the Bay of Bengal.\nRiver length is a common Indian geography matching topic."},
{"no":79,"text":"If the radius of the Earth were shrunk by 1%, while its mass remained the same, the value of g on Earth's surface would:","A":"Increase by 0.5%","B":"Increase by 2%","C":"Decrease by 0.5%","D":"Decrease by 2%","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Physics","tags":["Gravity","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"Surface gravity:g = GM/R²\nMass remains constant.\nIf radius decreases by 1%:\nNew radius = 0.99R\nTherefore:\nNew g = GM/(0.99R)²\nSince gravity varies inversely with R², a 1% reduction in radius produces approximately a 2% increase in g.","revisionPoints":"g = GM/R².\ng increases when:\nMass increases\nRadius decreases\nFor small percentage changes:\n1% decrease in radius → approximately 2% increase in g.\nThis is an application of inverse-square law."},
{"no":80,"text":"With reference to the entry of European powers into India, which one of the following statements is correct?","A":"The Portuguese captured Goa in 1499.","B":"The English opened their first factory in South India at Masulipatnam.","C":"In Eastern India, the English Company opened its first factory in Orissa in 1633.","D":"Under the leadership of Dupleix, the French occupied Madras in 1746.","ans":"A","category":"History","subcategory":"Colonial History","tags":["European Colonial Powers","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"The source marks (a) as correct.\nHowever, the date in option (a) is historically problematic: the Portuguese conquest of Goa occurred in 1510 under Afonso de Albuquerque.\nThe source's answer key therefore contains an apparent error.\nOption (d) refers to the French capture of Madras in 1746, an important event during the Carnatic Wars.","revisionPoints":"Portuguese → first major European power to establish dominance in parts of coastal India.\nAfonso de Albuquerque → Goa, 1510.\nFrench captured Madras in 1746.\nEuropean powers in India:\nPortuguese\nDutch\nEnglish\nFrench\nDanish"},
{"no":81,"text":"With reference to the Indian freedom struggle, which one of the following statements is correct?","A":"The Rowlatt Act aroused a wave of popular indignation and led to the Jallianwala Bagh massacre.","B":"Subhash Chandra Bose formed the Forward Bloc.","C":"Bhagat Singh was one of the founders of Hindustan Republican Socialist Association.","D":"In 1931, the Congress Session at Karachi opposed the Gandhi-Irwin Pact.","ans":"D","category":"History","subcategory":"Freedom Movement","tags":["Karachi Session","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"The source marks (d) as the answer.\nHowever, this source key is problematic.\nThe Karachi Congress Session of 1931 approved/ratified the Gandhi-Irwin Pact rather than opposing it.\nSubhas Chandra Bose formed the Forward Bloc in 1939, making statement (b) historically important.","revisionPoints":"Rowlatt Act → 1919.\nJallianwala Bagh massacre → 13 April 1919.\nForward Bloc → Subhas Chandra Bose, 1939.\nKarachi Congress Session → 1931.\nKarachi Session is also important for the Fundamental Rights and National Economic Programme resolution."},
{"no":82,"text":"Consider the following statements:\n1. In the last five years, Indian software exports increased at a compound annual growth rate of about 60%.\n2. The software and service industry in India registered an overall growth of about 28% in rupee terms during 2001–02.\nWhich of these statements is/are correct?","A":"Only 1","B":"Only 2","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"IT & Services","tags":["Software Exports","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"The source marks both statements incorrect.\nThe question refers to historical IT-sector growth data.\nSuch numerical statements must be evaluated against the specific period mentioned.","revisionPoints":"Software exports became an important component of India's services exports.\nIT and IT-enabled services contributed significantly to India's external sector.\nDistinguish between:\nSoftware exports\nIT industry growth\nServices exports\nHistorical economic data are year-specific."},
{"no":83,"text":"Which one of the following statements is correct?","A":"India is the second largest producer of nitrogenous fertilisers in the world.","B":"India is the ninth largest steel producing country in the world.","C":"India is the second largest producer of silk in the world.","D":"India ranks third in the world in coal production.","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Industry","tags":["India Rankings","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"The source identifies (a) as correct.\nThe question tests India's position in major industrial and mineral sectors.\nIndia's fertiliser industry has historically been important because of the country's large agricultural sector.","revisionPoints":"Nitrogenous fertilisers primarily supply nitrogen.\nMajor nitrogen fertiliser: urea.\nFertiliser consumption is closely related to agricultural productivity.\nIndustrial rankings depend on the reference year."},
{"no":84,"text":"Which one of the following statements is correct?","A":"Only the Rajya Sabha and not the Lok Sabha can have nominated members.","B":"There is a constitutional provision for nominating two members belonging to the Anglo-Indian community to the Rajya Sabha.","C":"There is no constitutional bar for a nominated member to be appointed Union Minister.","D":"A nominated member can vote both in the Presidential election and other elections.","ans":"C","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Parliament","tags":["Nominated Members","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"A nominated member can become a Union Minister.\nHowever, nominated members of Parliament have restrictions regarding voting in certain elections.\nNominated members of Rajya Sabha participate in parliamentary proceedings but do not have the same voting rights in the Presidential election as elected MPs.","revisionPoints":"Rajya Sabha has 12 nominated members under Article 80.\nNominees are persons with special knowledge or practical experience in:\nLiterature\nScience\nArt\nSocial service\nNominated Rajya Sabha members cannot vote in the election of the President.\nA nominated member can become a Minister."},
{"no":85,"text":"With reference to Government of India's decisions regarding FDI during 2001–02, consider:\n1. Out of the 100% FDI allowed in the tea sector, the foreign firm would have to disinvest 33% equity in favour of an Indian partner within four years.\n2. Regarding FDI in print media, the single largest Indian shareholder should have a holding higher than 26%.\nWhich is/are correct?","A":"Only 1","B":"Only 2","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Foreign Direct Investment","tags":["FDI","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"The source identifies statement 2 only as correct.\nThe question relates specifically to the FDI policy prevailing in 2001–02.\nFDI rules differed according to the sector.","revisionPoints":"FDI policy can differ by sector.\nPrint media has historically had stricter foreign ownership restrictions.\nAlways distinguish FDI caps from automatic-route provisions.\nHistorical FDI questions should be answered according to the policy year specified."},
{"no":86,"text":"The leader of the Bardoli Satyagraha (1928) was:","A":"Sardar Vallabhbhai Patel","B":"Mahatma Gandhi","C":"Vithalbhai J. Patel","D":"Mahadev Desai","ans":"A","category":"History","subcategory":"Freedom Movement","tags":["Bardoli Satyagraha","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"The Bardoli Satyagraha took place in 1928 in Gujarat.\nIt was a protest against an increase in land revenue.\nVallabhbhai Patel led the movement.\nFollowing the successful movement, he received the title “Sardar.”","revisionPoints":"Bardoli Satyagraha → 1928.\nLeader → Vallabhbhai Patel.\nMain issue → Land revenue increase.\nLocation → Bardoli, Gujarat.\nImportant milestone in Patel's emergence as a national leader."},
{"no":87,"text":"The Kelkar proposals, which were in the news recently, were:","A":"Recommendations for reforms in the power sector","B":"Recommendations for tax reforms","C":"Guidelines for privatisation","D":"Guidelines for reducing vehicular pollution and promoting CNG use","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Taxation","tags":["Kelkar Committee","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"The Kelkar Committee was associated with tax reforms.\nThe recommendations covered areas relating to India's taxation system.\nThe question reflects an important economic-policy development of the period.","revisionPoints":"Kelkar Committee → Tax reforms.\nTax reforms can involve:\nDirect taxes\nIndirect taxes\nTax administration\nSimplification\nCommittee-based current affairs are frequently tested in UPSC."},
{"no":88,"text":"Consider the following statements:\n1. Joint sitting of the two Houses of Parliament is sanctioned under Article 108.\n2. The first joint sitting of Lok Sabha and Rajya Sabha was held in 1961.\n3. The second joint sitting was held to pass the Banking Service Commission (Repeal) Bill.\nWhich statements are correct?","A":"1 and 2","B":"2 and 3","C":"1 and 3","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Parliament","tags":["Joint Sitting","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"Article 108 provides for a joint sitting in certain cases of disagreement between the Houses.\nThe first joint sitting was held in 1961 for the Dowry Prohibition Bill.\nThe second joint sitting concerned the Banking Service Commission (Repeal) Bill.","revisionPoints":"Joint sitting → Article 108.\nPresided over by the Speaker of Lok Sabha.\nNot applicable to:\nMoney Bills\nConstitutional Amendment Bills\nIt is used to resolve certain deadlocks between the Houses."},
{"no":89,"text":"Life expectancy is highest in the world in:","A":"Canada","B":"Germany","C":"Japan","D":"Norway","ans":"C","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Human Development","tags":["Life Expectancy","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"The source identifies Japan.\nJapan has historically been known for very high life expectancy.\nFactors include healthcare access, nutrition and broader socioeconomic conditions.","revisionPoints":"Life expectancy → average expected years of life.\nIt is a major health and development indicator.\nJapan has historically ranked among countries with the highest life expectancy.\nLife expectancy is one component related to the health dimension of human development."},
{"no":90,"text":"During the colonial period in India, what was the purpose of the Whitley Commission?","A":"To review India's fitness for further political reforms","B":"To report on existing conditions of labour and make recommendations","C":"To draw up a plan for financial reforms for India","D":"To develop a comprehensive scheme for Civil Services in India","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"British Administration","tags":["Whitley Commission","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"The Whitley Commission was concerned with labour conditions.\nIt examined labour-related problems and made recommendations.\nIt was part of the colonial government's efforts concerning industrial labour.","revisionPoints":"Whitley Commission → Labour conditions.\nLabour commissions examined:\nWorking conditions\nWages\nIndustrial relations\nLabour welfare\nDo not confuse it with constitutional or civil-service commissions."},
{"no":91,"text":"Octopus is:","A":"An arthropod","B":"An echinoderm","C":"A hemichordate","D":"A mollusc","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biology","tags":["Octopus","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"Octopus belongs to the phylum Mollusca.\nIt belongs to the class Cephalopoda.\nOther cephalopods include:\nSquid\nCuttlefish\nNautilus","revisionPoints":"Octopus → Phylum Mollusca.\nClass → Cephalopoda.\nCephalopods are highly developed marine molluscs.\nThey possess:\nTentacles\nWell-developed eyes\nAdvanced nervous system"},
{"no":92,"text":"Consider the following conditions of a sick human body:\n1. Swollen lymph nodes\n2. Sweating at night\n3. Loss of memory\n4. Loss of weight\nWhich of these are symptoms of AIDS?","A":"1 and 2","B":"2, 3 and 4","C":"1, 2 and 3","D":"1, 2, 3 and 4","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Health & Diseases","tags":["AIDS","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"The source identifies all four as associated symptoms.\nHIV/AIDS can affect the immune system and produce a range of systemic manifestations.\nAdvanced disease can involve neurological complications as well.","revisionPoints":"AIDS → Acquired Immunodeficiency Syndrome.\nCaused by HIV.\nHIV attacks the immune system, particularly CD4 T cells.\nPossible manifestations include:\nWeight loss\nPersistent infections\nSwollen lymph nodes\nNight sweats"},
{"no":93,"text":"Emperor Harsha's southward march was stopped on the Narmada River by:","A":"Pulakesin I","B":"Pulakesin II","C":"Vikramaditya I","D":"Vikramaditya II","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Ancient India","tags":["Harsha","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"Harshavardhana attempted to expand southward.\nHis advance was checked by Pulakesin II of the Chalukyas.\nThe Narmada became an important boundary between their spheres of influence.","revisionPoints":"Harshavardhana → Pushyabhuti/Vardhana dynasty.\nPulakesin II → Chalukya ruler of Badami.\nHarsha's southern expansion was checked near the Narmada.\nChinese traveller Xuanzang (Hiuen Tsang) visited Harsha's court."},
{"no":94,"text":"Under which Article of the Indian Constitution did the President give assent to an ordinance on electoral reforms when it was sent back to him by the Union Cabinet without changes in 2002?","A":"Article 121","B":"Article 122","C":"Article 123","D":"Article 124","ans":"C","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Constitution","tags":["Ordinance Making Power","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"Article 123 gives the President power to promulgate Ordinances when Parliament is not in session.\nAn ordinance has the same force and effect as an Act of Parliament, subject to constitutional limitations.\nTherefore, the relevant Article is 123.","revisionPoints":"Article 123 → President's Ordinance-making power.\nArticle 213 → Governor's Ordinance-making power.\nOrdinance-making power is exercised when Parliament is not in session.\nOrdinances must eventually be placed before Parliament."},
{"no":95,"text":"Dilip Kaur Tiwana is well known as a:","A":"Litterateur","B":"Musician","C":"Scientist","D":"Sports person","ans":"A","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Personalities","tags":["Litterateurs","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"Dilip Kaur Tiwana was a prominent Punjabi writer.\nShe is associated particularly with Punjabi literature.\nHer literary work includes novels and short stories.","revisionPoints":"Dilip Kaur Tiwana → Punjabi literature.\n“Litterateur” means a person engaged in literary work.\nRegional-language literature is an important part of India's cultural diversity."},
{"no":96,"text":"Consider the following statements:\n1. The maximum limit of shareholding of Indian promoters in private sector banks in India is 49% of paid-up capital.\n2. FDI up to 49% from all sources is permitted in private sector banks in India under the automatic route.\nWhich of these statements is/are correct?","A":"Only 1","B":"Only 2","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Banking","tags":["Private Sector Banks","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"The source marks both statements as incorrect.\nThe question concerns banking ownership and FDI rules applicable during the period.\nSuch regulatory limits have changed over time, so the year/context is essential.","revisionPoints":"Private-sector banking regulation involves:\nRBI\nBanking Regulation Act\nFDI policy\nOwnership limits can change through government/RBI policy.\nAlways identify the time period before answering historical economic-policy questions."},
{"no":97,"text":"Match the following cricketers with their countries:\nCricketer\nCountry\nA. Barry Richards\n1. England\nB. Ted Dexter\n2. West Indies\nC. Alan Davidson\n3. South Africa\nD. Charlie Griffith\n4. New Zealand\n5. Australia\nCodes:","A":"3 2 5 1","B":"5 1 4 2","C":"3 1 5 2","D":"5 2 4 1","ans":"C","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Sports","tags":["Cricket","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"Barry Richards → South Africa\nTed Dexter → England\nAlan Davidson → Australia\nCharlie Griffith → West Indies\nTherefore: 3–1–5–2.","revisionPoints":"Barry Richards → South Africa.\nTed Dexter → England.\nAlan Davidson → Australia.\nCharlie Griffith → West Indies.\nMatch-the-following questions often test sports personalities and countries."},
{"no":98,"text":"Regarding the atom of a chemical element, the magnetic quantum number refers to:","A":"Orientation","B":"Shape","C":"Size","D":"Spin","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Chemistry","tags":["Quantum Numbers","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"The source marks (d).\nHowever, scientifically, the magnetic quantum number (mₗ) describes the orientation of an orbital in space.\nThe spin quantum number (mₛ) describes electron spin.\nTherefore, the source answer appears to contain an error.","revisionPoints":"Principal quantum number (n) → shell/energy level.\nAzimuthal quantum number (l) → subshell/orbital shape.\nMagnetic quantum number (mₗ) → orbital orientation."},
{"no":99,"text":"Consider the following statements:\n1. The common blue-green algae Spirogyra and Ulothrix are found in both freshwater ponds and oceans.\n2. A chameleon can look ahead with one eye and at the same time look behind with another.\nWhich of these statements is/are correct?","A":"Only 1","B":"Only 2","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biology","tags":["Chameleon","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"The source identifies only statement 2 as correct.\nA chameleon's eyes can move independently, allowing it to observe different directions simultaneously.\nThe statement regarding both Spirogyra and Ulothrix occurring in freshwater and oceans is not accepted by the source.","revisionPoints":"Chameleon eyes can move independently.\nSpirogyra → commonly associated with freshwater habitats.\nUlothrix → filamentous green alga, generally associated with freshwater.\nBiological questions often test adaptations and habitats."},
{"no":100,"text":"Which among the following countries had the largest population?","A":"Indonesia","B":"Japan","C":"Pakistan","D":"Sudan","ans":"A","category":"Geography","subcategory":"World Geography","tags":["World Population","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"According to the demographic situation referred to in the source, Indonesia had the largest population among the given options.\nJapan, Pakistan and Sudan had smaller populations in the relevant period.","revisionPoints":"Indonesia is the world's largest island country.\nIt has historically been the most populous country in Southeast Asia.\nPopulation rankings are time-sensitive.\nPopulation questions should always be interpreted according to the reference year."},
{"no":101,"text":"Assertion (A): Emperor Akbar marched towards Afghanistan in 1581 with a huge army.Reason (R): He was on his way to reclaim his ancestral country of Ferghana in Central Asia.","A":"Both A and R are individually true and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are individually true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true","ans":"C","category":"History","subcategory":"Medieval India","tags":["Akbar","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"Akbar marched towards Afghanistan in 1581.\nHis objective was connected with dealing with the political situation in Afghanistan and the north-west.\nThe reason given about reclaiming Ferghana is not correct.","revisionPoints":"Akbar → Mughal emperor.\nAkbar's reign → 1556–1605.\nAfghanistan was strategically important for the north-western frontier.\nFerghana was associated with Babur's Central Asian ancestry."},
{"no":102,"text":"Assertion (A): During 2001–02, the value of India's total exports declined, registering negative growth of 2.17%.\nReason (R): During 2001–02, negative growth in exports was witnessed in iron and steel, coffee, textiles and marine products.","A":"Both A and R are individually true and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are individually true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"External Sector","tags":["India's Exports","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"The source considers the stated 2.17% negative growth in Assertion A incorrect.\nIt considers the sectors mentioned in Reason R to have experienced negative export growth.\nTherefore, according to the source, A is false and R is true.","revisionPoints":"Export performance is influenced by:\nGlobal demand\nCommodity prices\nDomestic production\nExchange rates\nAlways distinguish total export growth from growth of individual commodities.\nEconomic assertions should be evaluated for the specific financial year."},
{"no":103,"text":"Assertion (A): The eastern coast of India produces more rice than the western coast.\nReason (R): The eastern coast receives more rainfall than the western coast.","A":"Both A and R are individually true and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are individually true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true","ans":"C","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography (India)","tags":["Rice Cultivation","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"The eastern coast is a major rice-producing region.\nThe western coast generally receives more rainfall than the eastern coast because of the southwest monsoon.\nTherefore, the Reason is false.\nRice production depends on more than rainfall, including soil, irrigation and agricultural conditions.","revisionPoints":"Major rice-producing regions → eastern and northeastern India, coastal areas and irrigated regions.\nWestern coast → receives very heavy southwest monsoon rainfall.\nWestern Ghats cause strong orographic rainfall.\nRainfall alone does not determine agricultural production."},
{"no":104,"text":"Assertion (A): The new EXIM Policy is liberal, market-oriented and favours global trade.\nReason (R): GATT has played a significant role in the liberalisation of the economy.","A":"Both A and R are individually true and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are individually true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"External Sector","tags":["EXIM Policy","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"The source considers both statements correct.\nIndia's EXIM policy became increasingly liberal and outward-oriented.\nGATT contributed significantly to international trade liberalisation.\nHowever, GATT alone does not fully explain India's EXIM policy.","revisionPoints":"EXIM → Export-Import Policy.\nGATT → General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade.\nGATT focused on reducing barriers to international trade.\nGATT was replaced by the WTO in 1995.\nTrade liberalisation promotes greater integration with the global economy."},
{"no":105,"text":"Assertion (A): In the periodic table, electron affinity is always found to increase from top to bottom in a group.\nReason (R): In a group, atomic radius generally increases from top to bottom.","A":"Both A and R are individually true and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are individually true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Chemistry","tags":["Periodic Table","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"Atomic radius generally increases down a group because additional electron shells are added.\nElectron affinity does not always increase down a group.\nIncreasing atomic size and shielding affect the attraction between the nucleus and an added electron.","revisionPoints":"Atomic radius → generally increases down a group.\nIonisation energy → generally decreases down a group.\nElectronegativity → generally decreases down a group.\nPeriodic trends can have important exceptions."},
{"no":106,"text":"Assertion (A): Coal-based thermal power stations contribute to acid rain.\nReason (R): Oxides of carbon are emitted when coal burns.","A":"Both A and R are individually true and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are individually true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true","ans":"A","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Pollution","tags":["Acid Rain","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"Coal combustion releases various gases.\nThermal power plants can contribute to air pollution and acid deposition.\nHowever, acid rain is principally associated with sulphur oxides and nitrogen oxides, rather than carbon oxides.\nThus, the source's key uses a simplified/incorrect chemical explanation.","revisionPoints":"Acid rain mainly involves:\nSO₂ / SOₓ\nNOₓ\nSO₂ can form sulphuric acid.\nNOₓ can contribute to formation of nitric acid.\nCoal combustion is an important source of air pollutants."},
{"no":107,"text":"Assertion (A): The weight of a body decreases with increase of latitude on Earth.\nReason (R): The Earth is not a perfect sphere.","A":"Both A and R are individually true and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are individually true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Physics","tags":["Gravity","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"Weight generally increases with latitude, rather than decreases.\nEffective gravity is lower near the equator and higher near the poles.\nEarth is not a perfect sphere; it is an oblate spheroid.\nTherefore:\nAssertion → False\nReason → True","revisionPoints":"Effective g is minimum near the equator.\nEffective g is maximum near the poles.\nReasons include:\nEarth's rotation\nEarth's oblate shape\nEarth is slightly flattened at the poles."},
{"no":108,"text":"Assertion (A): Unsaturated fats are more reactive compared with saturated fats.\nReason (R): Unsaturated fats have only single bonds in their structure.","A":"Both A and R are individually true and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are individually true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Chemistry","tags":["Fats","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"Unsaturated fats contain one or more carbon-carbon double bonds.\nThese double bonds make them more chemically reactive than saturated fats.\nTherefore, Assertion is true.\nThe Reason is false because saturated fats, not unsaturated fats, have only single C–C bonds.","revisionPoints":"Saturated fatty acids → no C=C double bonds.\nUnsaturated fatty acids → one or more C=C bonds.\nUnsaturated fats are generally more susceptible to oxidation.\nHydrogenation can reduce unsaturation."},
{"no":109,"text":"Assertion (A): The amount of moisture in the atmosphere is related to latitude.\nReason (R): The capacity to hold moisture in the form of water vapour is related to temperature.","A":"Both A and R are individually true and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are individually true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true","ans":"A","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Climatology","tags":["Atmospheric Moisture","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"Atmospheric moisture varies with latitude.\nTemperature strongly influences the atmosphere's capacity to hold water vapour.\nWarmer air can hold more water vapour than colder air.\nHence temperature is an important reason for the latitudinal variation of atmospheric moisture.","revisionPoints":"Atmospheric moisture includes water vapour.\nWarm air → greater moisture-holding capacity.\nEquatorial regions → generally high humidity.\nPolar regions → very low atmospheric moisture.\nHumidity is important for cloud formation and precipitation."},
{"no":110,"text":"Assertion (A): Unlike temperate forests, tropical rain forests, if cleared, can yield productive farmland capable of supporting intensive agriculture for several years even without chemical fertilisers.\nReason (R): Primary productivity of tropical rain forests is very high compared with temperate forests.","A":"Both A and R are individually true and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are individually true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true","ans":"D","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Ecology","tags":["Tropical Rain Forests","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"Tropical rainforests have very high primary productivity.\nHowever, much of the nutrient cycling is rapid and nutrients are concentrated in biomass, rather than deep fertile soil.\nClearing the forest can quickly lead to nutrient depletion and soil degradation.\nTherefore, the Assertion is false while the Reason is true.","revisionPoints":"Tropical rainforests → high biodiversity and high productivity.\nNutrient cycling is rapid.\nForest clearing can cause:\nSoil erosion\nNutrient loss\nReduced long-term fertility\nHigh biomass does not necessarily mean high agricultural soil fertility."},
{"no":111,"text":"Assertion (A): In 1916, Maulana Mohammad Ali and Abul Kalam Azad resigned from the Legislative Council.\nReason (R): The Rowlatt Act was passed by the government despite being opposed by all Indian members of the Legislative Council.","A":"Both A and R are individually true and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are individually true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true","ans":"D","category":"History","subcategory":"Freedom Movement","tags":["Rowlatt Act","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"The source marks A false and R true.\nThe Rowlatt legislation belongs to 1919, not 1916.\nThe question therefore involves a chronology trap.\nThe source's answer should be retained for source-based practice.","revisionPoints":"Rowlatt Act → 1919.\nAlso known as the Anarchical and Revolutionary Crimes Act.\nIt allowed detention without normal trial safeguards.\nStrongly opposed by Indian nationalists.\nGandhi launched the Rowlatt Satyagraha."},
{"no":112,"text":"Assertion (A): Shah Alam II spent the initial years as an Emperor far away from his capital.\nReason (R): There was always a lurking danger of foreign invasion from the north-western frontier.","A":"Both A and R are individually true and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are individually true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Medieval India","tags":["Shah Alam II","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"Shah Alam II initially remained away from Delhi.\nThe political circumstances of the Mughal Empire were highly unstable.\nThe source considers the north-western invasion threat true but not the direct explanation for his absence from the capital.","revisionPoints":"Shah Alam II → Mughal emperor.\nReigned during the period of increasing Mughal decline.\nBattle of Buxar → 1764.\nTreaty of Allahabad → 1765.\nMughal political authority increasingly weakened during the 18th century."},
{"no":113,"text":"Assertion (A): Areas lying within five to eight degrees latitude on either side of the equator receive rainfall throughout the year.\nReason (R): High temperatures and high humidity cause convectional rain to fall mostly in the afternoons near the equator.","A":"Both A and R are individually true and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are individually true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true","ans":"A","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Climatology","tags":["Equatorial Climate","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"Equatorial regions receive rainfall throughout much of the year.\nStrong heating produces convectional uplift.\nHigh temperature and humidity promote cloud formation.\nConvectional rainfall is often associated with afternoon thunderstorms.","revisionPoints":"Equatorial climate → hot and wet throughout the year.\nStrong convection → frequent rainfall.\nHigh humidity → abundant atmospheric moisture.\nEquatorial rainfall is often called convectional rainfall."},
{"no":114,"text":"Assertion (A): Saluva Narasimha put an end to the old dynasty and assumed the royal title.\nReason (R): He wanted to save the kingdom from further degeneration and disintegration.","A":"Both A and R are individually true and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are individually true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true","ans":"A","category":"History","subcategory":"Medieval India","tags":["Vijayanagara Empire","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"Saluva Narasimha established the Saluva dynasty of Vijayanagara.\nHe took power when the Vijayanagara Empire was facing internal weakness.\nHis action was aimed at preventing further decline and disintegration.","revisionPoints":"Vijayanagara Empire → founded in 1336.\nSaluva Narasimha → associated with the Saluva dynasty.\nVijayanagara had several ruling dynasties.\nMajor dynasties:\nSangama\nSaluva\nTuluva\nAravidu"},
{"no":115,"text":"Assertion (A): Marathas emerged as the strongest native power in India after the decline of the Mughal Empire.\nReason (R): Marathas were the first to have a clear concept of a united Indian nation.","A":"Both A and R are individually true and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are individually true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true","ans":"C","category":"History","subcategory":"Medieval India","tags":["Maratha Empire","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"The Marathas became a major political and military power following Mughal decline.\nHowever, describing them as the first to have a clear concept of a united Indian nation is historically inappropriate.\nTherefore, the Assertion is true while the Reason is false.","revisionPoints":"Maratha power expanded significantly under Shivaji and later rulers.\nShivaji → founder of an independent Maratha kingdom.\nMarathas became a major power in 18th-century India.\nPeshwas played an important role in later Maratha expansion."},
{"no":116,"text":"Which one of the following countries does NOT border the Caspian Sea?","A":"Armenia","B":"Azerbaijan","C":"Kazakhstan","D":"Turkmenistan","ans":"A","category":"Geography","subcategory":"World Geography","tags":["Caspian Sea","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"The Caspian Sea is bordered by five countries:\nRussia\nKazakhstan\nTurkmenistan\nIran\nAzerbaijan\nArmenia is landlocked and does not border the Caspian Sea.","revisionPoints":"Caspian Sea → world's largest inland body of water.\nFive littoral states:\nRussia\nKazakhstan\nTurkmenistan\nIran\nAzerbaijan\nArmenia is located west of Azerbaijan and is landlocked."},
{"no":117,"text":"The sportsperson Soma Biswas is associated with:","A":"Sailing","B":"Hockey","C":"Golf","D":"Athletics","ans":"D","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Sports","tags":["Athletics","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"Soma Biswas is associated with athletics.\nShe represented India in track and field events.","revisionPoints":"Soma Biswas → Athletics.\nAthletics includes:\nTrack events\nField events\nCombined events\nSports personalities were frequently included in UPSC current-affairs questions of this period."},
{"no":118,"text":"Which one of the following is produced during the formation of photochemical smog?","A":"Hydrocarbons","B":"Nitrogen Oxides","C":"Ozone","D":"Methane","ans":"C","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Pollution","tags":["Photochemical Smog","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"Photochemical smog forms when pollutants react in the presence of sunlight.\nNitrogen oxides and hydrocarbons/VOCs participate in photochemical reactions.\nThese reactions produce ground-level ozone and other secondary pollutants.","revisionPoints":"Photochemical smog → associated with sunlight + NOₓ + hydrocarbons/VOCs.\nMajor secondary pollutant → ground-level ozone.\nOzone in the stratosphere is protective.\nOzone near the ground is an air pollutant."},
{"no":119,"text":"Consider the following statements:\n1. Kitab-i-Nauras, a collection of songs in praise of Hindu deities and Muslim saints, was written by Ibrahim Adil Shah II.\n2. Amir Khusrau was the originator in India of the early form of the musical style known as Qawwali.\nWhich of these statements is/are correct?","A":"Only 1","B":"Only 2","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"C","category":"History","subcategory":"Medieval India","tags":["Kitab-I-Nauras","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"Ibrahim Adil Shah II of Bijapur authored Kitab-i-Nauras.\nThe work reflects a synthesis of Hindu and Islamic cultural traditions.\nAmir Khusrau is traditionally associated with the early development of Qawwali and several Indo-Persian musical traditions.","revisionPoints":"Ibrahim Adil Shah II → Adil Shahi dynasty, Bijapur.\nKitab-i-Nauras → music and devotional compositions.\nAmir Khusrau → major figure of Indo-Islamic cultural synthesis.\nQawwali → devotional musical tradition associated with Sufi culture."},
{"no":120,"text":"Three bells toll at intervals of 9, 12 and 15 minutes respectively. All three begin to toll at 8:00 a.m. At what time will they first toll together again?","A":"8:45 a.m.","B":"10:30 a.m.","C":"11:00 a.m.","D":"1:30 p.m.","ans":"C","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Quantitative Aptitude","tags":["UPSC","2003"],"expl":"We need the LCM of 9, 12 and 15.\nPrime factors:\n9 = 3²\n12 = 2² × 3\n15 = 3 × 5\nLCM = 2² × 3² × 5 = 180 minutes.\n180 minutes = 3 hours.\n8:00 a.m. + 3 hours = 11:00 a.m.","revisionPoints":"Repeated-event problems → use LCM.\n9, 12, 15 → LCM = 180 minutes.\n180 minutes = 3 hours.\nFirst simultaneous toll after starting time → 11:00 a.m."},
{"no":121,"text":"Match the following distinguished women with their organisations/industries:\nDistinguished Lady\nOrganisation/Industry\nA. Amrita\n1. National Dairy Development Board\nB. Anu Aga\n2. Park Hotels\nC. Mallika Srinivasan\n3. Pfizer Limited\nD. Priya Paul\n4. Thermax Limited\n5. Tractors and Farm Equipment Limited\nCodes:","A":"1 4 5 2","B":"2 4 1 5","C":"1 3 5 2","D":"2 3 1 5","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Business Personalities","tags":["Notable Women","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"Amrita → National Dairy Development Board\nAnu Aga → Thermax Limited\nMallika Srinivasan → Tractors and Farm Equipment Limited\nPriya Paul → Park Hotels\nTherefore, the correct sequence is 1–4–5–2.","revisionPoints":"Anu Aga → Thermax.\nMallika Srinivasan → TAFE.\nPriya Paul → Park Hotels.\nMatch-the-following questions often test prominent personalities with institutions."},
{"no":122,"text":"Consider the following statements regarding the electoral college for the Presidential Election in India:\n1. The value of the vote of an elected Member of Legislative Assembly equals ______.\n2. The value of the vote of an elected Member of Parliament equals ______.\n3. There were more than 5000 members in the latest election.\nWhich of these statements is/are correct?","A":"1 and 2","B":"Only 2","C":"1 and 3","D":"Only 3","ans":"B","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Presidential Election","tags":["Electoral College","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"The source's OCR has missing portions in statements 1 and 2.\nThe source nevertheless marks (b) as the answer.\nPresidential election voting uses a weighted vote system, rather than one person–one vote.","revisionPoints":"Presidential electoral college includes:\nElected MPs of both Houses.\nElected MLAs of States.\nNominated MPs/MLAs do not participate.\nValue of votes is determined constitutionally.\nPresidential election → proportional representation by single transferable vote."},
{"no":123,"text":"Which one of the following statements is NOT correct?","A":"Ali Mardan Khan introduced the system of revenue farming in Bengal.","B":"Maharaja Ranjit Singh set up modern foundries to manufacture cannons at Lahore.","C":"Sawai Jai Singh of Amber had Euclid's Elements of Geometry translated into Sanskrit.","D":"Sultan Tipu of Mysore gave money for construction of the idol of Goddess Sharada in the Sringeri temple.","ans":"A","category":"History","subcategory":"Medieval India","tags":["Regional Kingdoms","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"The source identifies (a) as incorrect.\nThe other statements describe historical developments associated with:\nRanjit Singh\nSawai Jai Singh\nTipu Sultan\nTipu Sultan's association with the Sringeri temple is an important example of complex political and cultural relations of the period.","revisionPoints":"Ranjit Singh → Lahore / Sikh Empire.\nSawai Jai Singh II → astronomy and scientific instruments.\nTipu Sultan → Mysore.\nSringeri is an important centre of Advaita tradition."},
{"no":124,"text":"Which Constitutional Amendments are related to raising the number of Members of Lok Sabha to be elected from the States?","A":"6th and 22nd","B":"13th and 38th","C":"7th and 31st","D":"11th and 42nd","ans":"C","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Constitutional Amendments","tags":["Lok Sabha Seats","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"The source identifies the 7th and 31st Constitutional Amendments.\nThese amendments dealt with changes connected with the representation and composition of Parliament.","revisionPoints":"7th Amendment, 1956 → major changes related to States and territories.\n31st Amendment, 1973 → increased representation in Lok Sabha.\nConstitutional Amendments are frequently tested through year + subject matching."},
{"no":125,"text":"How did Sultan Qutbuddin Aibak die?","A":"He was treacherously stabbed to death by one of his ambitious nobles.","B":"He was killed in a battle with Tajuddin Yildiz, ruler of Ghazni.","C":"He sustained injuries while besieging Kalinjar and later died.","D":"He died after falling from his horse while playing Chaugan.","ans":"D","category":"History","subcategory":"Medieval India","tags":["Delhi Sultanate","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"Qutbuddin Aibak died in 1210 CE.\nHe suffered injuries after falling from his horse while playing Chaugan (polo).\nHe was the founder of the Mamluk/Slave dynasty in India.","revisionPoints":"Qutbuddin Aibak → founder of Delhi Sultanate's Mamluk dynasty.\nReign → 1206–1210.\nKnown as Lakh Baksh.\nDied while playing Chaugan.\nIltutmish succeeded him."},
{"no":126,"text":"Among the following, which planet takes the maximum time for one revolution around the Sun?","A":"Earth","B":"Jupiter","C":"Mars","D":"Venus","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Astronomy","tags":["Solar System","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"The orbital period increases with distance from the Sun.\nAmong the given planets, Jupiter is farthest from the Sun.\nJupiter takes approximately 11.86 Earth years to complete one revolution.","revisionPoints":"Earth → approximately 365.25 days.\nMars → approximately 687 days.\nJupiter → approximately 11.86 years.\nVenus → approximately 224.7 days.\nGreater orbital distance generally means a longer orbital period."},
{"no":127,"text":"Which one among the following languages has the largest number of speakers in the world?","A":"Bengali","B":"French","C":"Japanese","D":"Portuguese","ans":"A","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"General Knowledge","tags":["World Languages","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"The source identifies Bengali.\nThis is a historical source-based question, and the ranking depends on whether first-language speakers or total speakers are considered.\nThe source's answer should therefore be retained for this particular paper.","revisionPoints":"Bengali → major language of India and Bangladesh.\nFrench → widely spoken across multiple continents.\nPortuguese → major language of Brazil and Portugal.\nLanguage rankings depend on the definition and reference year."},
{"no":128,"text":"Which one of the following provisions was NOT made in the Charter Act of 1833?","A":"Trading activities of the East India Company were to be abolished.","B":"The designation of the supreme authority was changed to Governor-General of India in Council.","C":"All law-making powers were conferred on the Governor-General in Council.","D":"An Indian was to be appointed as a Law Member in the Governor-General's Council.","ans":"D","category":"History","subcategory":"British Administration","tags":["Charter Act 1833","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"The Charter Act of 1833 ended the East India Company's remaining commercial activities.\nIt made the Governor-General of Bengal the Governor-General of India.\nLegislative powers were centralised in the Governor-General in Council.\nThe Law Member was added to the Council, but the statement specifically claiming an Indian appointment was not a provision of the Act.","revisionPoints":"Charter Act → 1833.\nEast India Company's commercial role → ended.\nGovernor-General of Bengal → Governor-General of India.\nLegislative centralisation increased.\nImportant precursor to later constitutional developments."},
{"no":129,"text":"With reference to normal human beings, consider:\n1. In response to the presence of HCl, secretin is produced from the duodenum.\n2. Enterogastrone is produced in the small intestine in response to the presence of fatty acids.\nWhich of these statements is/are correct?","A":"Only 1","B":"Only 2","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biology","tags":["Digestive Hormones","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"Secretin is released by the duodenum in response to acidic chyme entering from the stomach.\nSecretin stimulates pancreatic bicarbonate secretion.\nEnterogastrone is associated with intestinal responses to fats and helps inhibit gastric activity.\nHence both statements are considered correct by the source.","revisionPoints":"Secretin → produced by duodenal mucosa.\nTrigger → acidic chyme/HCl.\nSecretin → stimulates bicarbonate-rich pancreatic secretion.\nEnterogastrone → associated with fat in the small intestine.\nThese are examples of gastrointestinal hormones."},
{"no":130,"text":"Survey of India is under the Ministry of:","A":"Defence","B":"Environment and Forests","C":"Home Affairs","D":"Science and Technology","ans":"D","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Government Bodies","tags":["Survey of India","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"The source identifies the Department of Science and Technology as the relevant ministry.\nSurvey of India is India's national surveying and mapping organisation.\nIts functions include mapping and geospatial surveying.","revisionPoints":"Survey of India → national surveying and mapping organisation.\nAssociated with Department of Science and Technology.\nImportant activities:\nTopographic mapping\nGeodetic surveying\nCartography\nMaps and surveys are essential for planning and administration."},
{"no":131,"text":"Which among the following countries was the earliest to give women the right to vote?","A":"Iceland","B":"India","C":"New Zealand","D":"USA","ans":"C","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"World History","tags":["Women's Suffrage","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"New Zealand became the first self-governing country to grant women the right to vote in 1893.\nIt was an important milestone in the global women's suffrage movement.","revisionPoints":"New Zealand → women's suffrage 1893.\nWomen's suffrage refers to the right to vote.\nIndia granted universal adult franchise with the Constitution coming into force in 1950.\nSuffrage history is an important comparative polity topic."},
{"no":132,"text":"Total time taken by a person going to a place by walking and returning by cycle is 5 hours 45 minutes. He would gain 2 hours by cycling both ways. The time taken by him to walk both ways is:","A":"6 hours 45 minutes","B":"7 hours 45 minutes","C":"8 hours 15 minutes","D":"8 hours 30 minutes","ans":"B","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Quantitative Aptitude","tags":["UPSC","2003"],"expl":"Let walking time for one way = W.\nCycling time for one way = C.\nWalking one way + cycling one way:\nW + C = 5 h 45 min\nCycling both ways saves 2 hours compared with walking + cycling:\n2C = (W + C) − 2h\n2C = 3h45m\nTherefore C = 1h52m30s.\nW = 5h45m − 1h52m30s = 3h52m30s.\nWalking both ways = 7h45m.","revisionPoints":"Convert mixed time units carefully.\n“Gain of 2 hours” means:\nOriginal time − faster journey time = 2 hours.\nThese questions can be solved using algebraic equations."},
{"no":133,"text":"Sandeep Pandey, the winner of the Ramon Magsaysay Award, is mainly an activist in:","A":"Prevention of child marriages","B":"Promotion of communal harmony","C":"Environmental protection","D":"Education and livelihood projects for Dalits","ans":"D","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Awards","tags":["Ramon Magsaysay Award","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"The source identifies (d).\nSandeep Pandey is associated with social activism and grassroots development work.\nHis work has included efforts related to education and disadvantaged communities.","revisionPoints":"Ramon Magsaysay Award → major Asian award.\nNamed after Ramon Magsaysay, former President of the Philippines.\nSandeep Pandey → social activist.\nSocial development questions often link personalities with their specific field of work."},
{"no":134,"text":"What was the main reason for the great civil strife in Argentina recently?","A":"Ethnic conflict between native Red Indian tribes and others","B":"Economic crisis due to huge public debt","C":"Issue of using native languages as medium of instruction","D":"Clashes between government troops and secessionist groups","ans":"B","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Countries in News","tags":["Argentina","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"Argentina experienced a severe economic and financial crisis around 2001–02.\nHigh public debt and financial instability were major factors.\nThe crisis resulted in widespread social and political unrest.","revisionPoints":"Argentina → South America.\n2001–02 crisis → severe economic and financial instability.\nPublic debt and financial-system problems were major factors.\nEconomic crises can lead to political instability and social unrest."},
{"no":135,"text":"With reference to colonial rule in India, what was sought by the Ilbert Bill of 1883?","A":"To bring Indians and Europeans on par regarding criminal jurisdiction of courts","B":"To impose severe restrictions on the freedom of the native press","C":"To encourage Indians to appear for civil service examinations in India","D":"To allow Indians to possess arms by amending the Arms Act","ans":"A","category":"History","subcategory":"British Administration","tags":["Ilbert Bill","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"The Ilbert Bill was introduced under Lord Ripon.\nIt sought to allow senior Indian judges to exercise criminal jurisdiction over European British subjects.\nEuropean opposition to the proposal was intense.","revisionPoints":"Ilbert Bill → 1883.\nViceroy → Lord Ripon.\nIssue → racial equality in judicial jurisdiction.\nOpposition exposed the racial attitudes of sections of the colonial population."},
{"no":136,"text":"An important aspect of the Cripps Mission of 1942 was:","A":"All Indian States should join the Indian Union as a condition for autonomy.","B":"Creation of an Indian Union with Dominion status soon after the Second World War.","C":"Indian participation in the British war effort in return for immediate full sovereign independence after the war.","D":"A constitution for the entire Indian Union with no separate provincial constitution.","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Freedom Movement","tags":["Cripps Mission","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"The Cripps Mission was sent to India in 1942 during World War II.\nIt proposed Dominion Status after the war.\nThe proposal also included provisions relating to constitution-making and provinces.\nIndian political leaders did not accept the proposal.","revisionPoints":"Cripps Mission → 1942.\nHeaded by Sir Stafford Cripps.\nOffer → Dominion Status after World War II.\nFailure of the mission was followed by the Quit India Movement, 1942."},
{"no":137,"text":"When Congress leaders condemned the Montagu-Chelmsford Report, many moderates left the party to form the:","A":"Swarajya Party","B":"Indian Freedom Party","C":"Independence Federation of India","D":"Indian Liberal Federation","ans":"D","category":"History","subcategory":"Freedom Movement","tags":["Indian Liberal Federation","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"The source identifies Indian Liberal Federation.\nIt represented the moderate/liberal political tendency that developed separately from the Congress mainstream.\nThe question reflects the ideological differences within Indian nationalism.","revisionPoints":"Montagu-Chelmsford reforms → associated with Government of India Act, 1919.\nModerates favoured constitutional and gradual political reforms.\nIndian Liberal Federation → associated with moderate/liberal politics.\nSwarajya Party was formed later by C.R. Das and Motilal Nehru."},
{"no":138,"text":"In the 2002 FIFA World Cup, how many goals did Ronaldo of Brazil score?","A":"6","B":"7","C":"8","D":"9","ans":"C","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Sports","tags":["FIFA World Cup","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"Ronaldo scored 8 goals in the 2002 FIFA World Cup.\nHe was the tournament's top scorer.\nBrazil won the tournament by defeating Germany in the final.","revisionPoints":"FIFA World Cup 2002 → South Korea and Japan.\nChampion → Brazil.\nTop scorer → Ronaldo, 8 goals.\nFinal → Brazil defeated Germany."},
{"no":139,"text":"Which Schedule of the Indian Constitution lists the names of States and specifies their territories?","A":"First","B":"Second","C":"Third","D":"Fourth","ans":"A","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Constitution","tags":["First Schedule","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"The First Schedule contains:\nNames of States\nNames of Union Territories\nTheir territorial extent\nTherefore, option (a) is correct.","revisionPoints":"First Schedule → States and Union Territories.\nSecond Schedule → salaries, allowances and privileges of constitutional authorities.\nThird Schedule → oaths and affirmations.\nFourth Schedule → allocation of Rajya Sabha seats."},
{"no":140,"text":"In 2002, the President of India presented the national award for excellence in public administration and management sciences to:","A":"Kumar Mangalam Birla","B":"N.R. Narayana Murthy","C":"Rahul Bajaj","D":"Ratan Tata","ans":"B","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Awards","tags":["National Awards","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"The source identifies N.R. Narayana Murthy.\nHe is associated with Infosys and the Indian information technology sector.\nThe question refers to a national award connected with excellence in management/public administration.","revisionPoints":"N.R. Narayana Murthy → Infosys.\nMajor contribution → Indian IT and software industry.\nManagement-related awards often test prominent Indian business leaders.\nDistinguish corporate achievements from government/public administration institutions."},
{"no":141,"text":"‘A’ and ‘B’ are two fixed points in a field. A cyclist C moves such that ∠ACB is always a right angle. Which statement is correct?","A":"The path followed by the cyclist is an ellipse","B":"The path followed by the cyclist is a circle","C":"The path followed by the cyclist is an exponential curve","D":"This type of motion is not possible","ans":"B","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Quantitative Aptitude","tags":["UPSC","2003"],"expl":"∠ACB is always 90°.\nA point from which a fixed line segment AB subtends a right angle lies on a circle.\nThis follows from Thales' theorem.\nTherefore, C moves along a circle having AB as diameter.","revisionPoints":"Thales' theorem: Angle in a semicircle is 90°.\nLocus of a point subtending a fixed line segment at 90° → circle.\nAB acts as the diameter of the circle."},
{"no":142,"text":"The thermal power plant of Bokaro is located in:","A":"Bihar","B":"Chhattisgarh","C":"Jharkhand","D":"Orissa","ans":"C","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Power Stations","tags":["Bokaro","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"Bokaro is located in the state of Jharkhand.\nJharkhand is an important coal-producing region of India.\nCoal availability supports the development of thermal power generation in the region.","revisionPoints":"Bokaro → Jharkhand.\nJharkhand → important coal-producing state.\nThermal power generation commonly depends on coal availability.\nBokaro is also known for its steel industry."},
{"no":143,"text":"Among the following commodities imported by India during 2001–02, which one was the highest in terms of Rupee value?","A":"Edible oil","B":"Fertilizers","C":"Organic and inorganic chemicals","D":"Pearls, precious and semiprecious stones","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"External Sector","tags":["India's Imports","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"The source identifies pearls, precious and semiprecious stones as the highest among the given options.\nThe question specifically asks for the value of imports in rupee terms, not physical quantity.\nTherefore, option (d) is correct.","revisionPoints":"Import statistics can be compared by:\nQuantity\nValue\nPercentage share\nPrecious and semiprecious stones were a significant import category.\nAlways distinguish value of trade from volume of trade."},
{"no":144,"text":"With reference to ionic compounds, consider:\n1. Ionic compounds are insoluble in alcohol.\n2. Ionic compounds in the solid state are good conductors of electricity.\nWhich of these statements is/are correct?","A":"Only 1","B":"Only 2","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"A","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Chemistry","tags":["Ionic Compounds","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"Statement 1 — Correct: Ionic compounds are generally insoluble or poorly soluble in alcohol.\nStatement 2 — Incorrect: In the solid state, ions are fixed in their lattice and cannot move freely.\nTherefore, solid ionic compounds do not conduct electricity effectively.\nThey generally conduct when molten or dissolved in water, because ions become mobile.","revisionPoints":"Ionic compounds contain cations and anions.\nSolid state → ions are fixed → poor electrical conductivity.\nMolten/aqueous state → ions are mobile → electrical conductivity.\nIonic compounds generally have:\nHigh melting points\nHigh boiling points\nCrystal lattice structure"},
{"no":145,"text":"Which one of the following statements is correct?","A":"Presence of NaCl increases the rate of setting of Plaster of Paris.","B":"Gypsum is added to cement to slow down its rate of setting.","C":"All alkaline earth metals form hydrated salts.","D":"Barium and Strontium are found free in nature.","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Chemistry","tags":["Alkaline Earth Metals","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"Gypsum is added during cement manufacture to regulate and slow the setting process.\nThis provides sufficient working time before the cement hardens.\nThe source's printed key marks (d), but that statement is chemically incorrect because barium and strontium are not found free in nature due to their reactivity.\nTherefore, based on the actual chemistry of the options, (b) is the correct statement.","revisionPoints":"Gypsum → CaSO₄·2H₂O.\nAdded to cement → controls/slows setting.\nAlkaline earth metals → Group 2 elements.\nBarium and strontium are reactive and occur mainly as compounds/minerals."},
{"no":146,"text":"With reference to India, which one of the following statements is not correct?","A":"IPCL is India's largest petrochemical company.","B":"RIL is the largest private sector company in India.","C":"MTNL is listed on NYSE.","D":"NSNL is the first telecom service organization in India to launch a nationwide cellular service at one time.","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Corporate Sector","tags":["Indian Companies","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"The source identifies (c) as the incorrect statement.\nThe question concerns major Indian companies and telecom organisations.\nMTNL's corporate listing history makes this statement the incorrect one according to the source.","revisionPoints":"IPCL → Indian Petrochemicals Corporation Limited.\nRIL → Reliance Industries Limited.\nMTNL → Mahanagar Telephone Nigam Limited.\nCorporate questions can change with mergers, ownership and market developments, so the year of the question matters."},
{"no":147,"text":"Which among the following States had the highest population density as per Census 2001?","A":"Kerala","B":"Madhya Pradesh","C":"Uttar Pradesh","D":"West Bengal","ans":"D","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Census","tags":["Census 2001","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"According to Census 2001, West Bengal had the highest population density among the given options.\nPopulation density means the number of people living per unit area.\nWest Bengal's high population concentration was particularly associated with its fertile plains and urban-industrial regions.","revisionPoints":"Population density = Population ÷ Area.\nCensus 2001 → West Bengal had very high population density.\nKerala also had high density.\nMadhya Pradesh has a comparatively large geographical area, lowering its density."},
{"no":148,"text":"The Ninth Schedule to the Indian Constitution was added by:","A":"First Amendment","B":"Eighth Amendment","C":"Ninth Amendment","D":"Forty-second Amendment","ans":"A","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Constitutional Amendments","tags":["Ninth Schedule","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"The Ninth Schedule was introduced through the Constitution (First Amendment) Act, 1951.\nIt was initially intended to protect certain land reform laws from judicial challenges.\nArticle 31B provides constitutional protection to laws placed in the Ninth Schedule, subject to later judicial interpretation.","revisionPoints":"Ninth Schedule → introduced by First Constitutional Amendment, 1951.\nAssociated constitutional provision → Article 31B.\nInitially linked strongly with land reforms.\nThe Supreme Court later examined the extent of Ninth Schedule protection."},
{"no":149,"text":"Who headed the Interim Cabinet formed in 1946?","A":"Rajendra Prasad","B":"Jawaharlal Nehru","C":"Sardar Vallabhbhai Patel","D":"Rajagopalachari","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Freedom Movement","tags":["Interim Government","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"The Interim Government was formed in 1946.\nJawaharlal Nehru headed it as Vice-President of the Viceroy's Executive Council.\nIt functioned as an important transitional government before independence.","revisionPoints":"Interim Government → 1946.\nHead → Jawaharlal Nehru.\nViceroy at the time → Lord Wavell initially.\nIt preceded the transfer of power and independence in 1947.\nThe Constituent Assembly also began functioning during this period."},
{"no":150,"text":"The waterfall Victoria Falls is associated with the river:","A":"Amazon","B":"Missouri","C":"St Lawrence","D":"Zambezi","ans":"D","category":"Geography","subcategory":"World Geography","tags":["Waterfalls","UPSC","2003"],"expl":"Victoria Falls is located on the Zambezi River.\nIt lies on the border between Zambia and Zimbabwe.\nThe falls are one of the world's major natural waterfalls.","revisionPoints":"Victoria Falls → Zambezi River.\nLocated between Zambia and Zimbabwe.\nZambezi → major river of southern Africa.\nOther important African rivers:\nNile\nCongo\nNiger\nZambezi"},
];
